// Kiddo School — Science, Nature & Discovery (Class 31) · Learning Path lessons.
// One individual flashcard teaching lesson per science card (owner doctrine:
// Learning Path = HOW to learn). Each lesson opens on its own /learn/{slug}/
// page: the original Canva flashcard on the left, this step-by-step teaching
// content on the right, NO drawing canvas anywhere. Every "notice" block
// references only what the artwork verifiably shows (per each card\u2019s alt
// text: child + subject + named props — open questions cover the rest, no
// invented details). Facts are grounded in the library card pages of
// data-science-nature-and-discovery.mjs but written fresh for teaching;
// safety fields appear only where a topic carries a real hazard (sun
// never-look, mushrooms never-eat, pond edges, volcano heat = grown-ups,
// bee calm distance, magnet small parts) and always state the rule WITH its
// reason — never a hollow reassurance.
export const lpContent={
'ant':{
 title:'Ants',one:'One crumb, many helpers — follow an ant line and meet the best team workers in the garden.',
 seoTitle:'Ant Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach Ant Teamwork (Class 31)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how ant teams share the work, a breadcrumb test to run on the pavement, and the habit of watching before concluding.',
 intro:'This lesson turns a pavement ant into a first science project: your child finds the trail, sets a tiny test, and watches a whole team answer the call — patience, prediction and teamwork, all in one crack in the pavement.',
 what:'Ant teamwork means jobs shared out: a scout finds food, leaves a smell trail on the way home, and the rest of the nest follows that scent line until every crumb is carried away.',
 mean:'No ant is giving orders; each one follows simple rules — follow the smell, pick up a piece, head home. From those small rules comes something big: a line of workers that behaves like one super-organism.',
 know:'Ants live in nests with a queen and thousands of workers. They lift loads far heavier than their own bodies, and the trails you see are scent roads — if a raindrop washes one away, the line falls apart until it is rebuilt.',
 why:'Watching ants teaches two science habits at once: look first, touch nothing, and let the evidence answer. It also plants the idea that small animals run on systems — a thought your child will reuse on bees, fish and even people.',
 dev:'Observation skills (watching without disturbing), prediction ("how many will come?"), counting, patience, and early systems thinking — many small workers, one big result.',
 vocab:'ant, trail, team, scent, carry, nest',
 teach:'1) Show the card and ask what the child in the picture is watching — and what the ant is carrying. Name the skill: watching a team at work.\n2) Find a real ant line on a pavement, wall or garden path. Hands behind backs: scientists watch, they do not poke.\n3) Set the crumb test: drop one small breadcrumb just off the path of the line and guess how long the first ant takes to find it.\n4) Count together as more ants arrive — first one, then three, then more than your child can count. Ask what told them where to come.\n5) End with the idea: the first scout left a smell road, and every ant that followed read it with feet and feelers. That is how a team talks without a single word.',
 say:'"One ant found this food and told the others with a smell line only ants can read. Now the whole team is carrying it home, piece by piece. Watch how they pass each other without bumping — every ant knows its job, and nobody is the boss."',
 notice:'Ask your child: who is the child watching, and what is the ant carrying? Is the leaf bigger than the ant itself? Where do you think the ant is taking it? Can you find where the ant line might be going?',
 ask:'"How did the first ant tell its friends about the food?" "What do ants carry food on?" "What happens if you put your finger on their path?" "Who is the boss of the ant team?"',
 childSay:'"Ants smell with their feet!" (close — the feelers and legs do the reading), "I counted twelve ants!" Your child may march in a line behind a sibling pretending to carry crumbs, or set a crumb test for ants on every walk.',
 real:'Ant teams show up everywhere: pavement cracks, picnic blankets, garden walls, the gap under a flowerpot. Once your child knows the trail rule, every walk becomes a chance to spot a team on shift — and to leave them to it.',
 play:'Crumb relay: you are the scout who finds a "crumb" (a block) across the room; your child smells the trail with a nose-wiggle, then the whole family forms a line and passes crumbs hand to hand to the "nest". Racing the clock makes the teamwork visible.',
 check:'Ask: "How do ants tell each other about food? Who is the boss? What do scientists do first — touch or watch?" Scent trail, no boss, watch first = landed.',
 tips:'Pick slow pavement edges rather than nests — disturbed ants swarm, which frightens small children. If your child wants to "help" the ants, suggest carrying the crumb test to them, not the ants to the crumb: it respects the workers and keeps fingers safe.'},
'bee':{
 title:'Bees',one:'A flower visitor with a job — how one small bee carries the golden dust that becomes seeds and fruit.',
 seoTitle:'Bee Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach Pollination (Class 31)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what a bee is doing inside a flower, a stand-back counting game, and the calm rules that keep everyone safe.',
 intro:'This lesson reframes the bee from something to fear into a worker with a delivery job: your child learns what the bee takes, what it leaves behind, and why the flowers in the garden are quietly saying thank you.',
 what:'Pollination means moving golden pollen dust from flower to flower. Bees do it by accident: they land to sip sweet nectar, pollen sticks to their fuzzy bodies, and some rubs off inside the next blossom.',
 mean:'The bee wants nectar, not seeds — but the pollen it carries is exactly what flowers need to make seeds and fruit. Two living things helping each other without either one planning it: that is partnership, and it feeds the whole garden.',
 know:'A bee can visit hundreds of blossoms in a single day. Pollen is a fine yellow dust made inside flowers; fruit — apples, strawberries, tomatoes — only forms after pollen reaches the right place.',
 why:'Bees connect animals to plants in your child\u2019s head: the apple on the table needed a bee last spring. That link grows respect for small creatures and answers the garden question "why do we never chase bees?" with real science, not just rules.',
 dev:'Careful observation at a distance, cause-and-effect thinking (bee visits, flower makes fruit), emotional learning about creatures that look alarming but mean no harm, and counting practice disguised as flower-watching.',
 vocab:'bee, flower, pollen, nectar, seeds, fruit',
 teach:'1) Show the card: what is the child watching, and what is the bee visiting? Name the job — this bee is a flower helper.\n2) Explain the two-part visit: the bee drinks sweet nectar deep in the flower, and golden pollen dust sticks to its fuzzy body like glitter.\n3) Count bee visits from a distance: pick two or three flowers in a garden or park, stand back, and tally which one the bees like best.\n4) Connect it to food: many fruits in the kitchen — apples, strawberries — grew only because a bee carried pollen last year.\n5) Practise the garden rule together: bees are busy, not mean. We stand still or walk calmly; we never swat or grab.',
 say:'"The bee is not bothering the flower — it is drinking sweet juice inside it. Look at its legs: golden dust! That dust is called pollen, and when the bee flies to the next flower, some dust falls off. That little dusting is how flowers make seeds and fruit."',
 notice:'Ask your child: what is the bee visiting? What is the child in the picture doing — running or watching? Where do you think the bee will fly next? Can you point to where the flowers are?',
 ask:'"What is the bee drinking inside the flower?" "What sticks to the bee\u2019s fuzzy body?" "What do flowers make after a bee visits?" "What do we do when a bee flies near — wave or stand calm?"',
 childSay:'"The bee is wearing pollen glitter!" "Do not chase bees — they are busy workers." Your child may count bee visits out loud in the garden, or explain to a worried friend that the bee is "just helping flowers make apples".',
 real:'Bee work is on every table: apples, berries, cucumbers and even the seeds inside tomatoes exist because bees moved pollen. The next fruit bowl becomes a review lesson — "which of these did bees help grow?" — and the answer is nearly all of them.',
 play:'Pollen glove game: put a sock or glove on your child\u2019s hand, dust it with flour "pollen", and have them touch five paper flowers around the room. Check each flower afterwards for flour — that is pollination, replayed by hand.',
 check:'Ask: "What does the bee drink? What sticks to its body? What do flowers make from pollen? What do we do near bees?" Nectar, pollen, seeds and fruit, stay calm = landed.',
 tips:'Watch from a step back rather than leaning in — bees sense sudden movement. Bare feet and flower beds do not mix: bees land on clover low in the grass, which is exactly where bare toes go.',
 safety:'Bees sting only when scared or squeezed — a calm child standing still is safe, and a bee buzzing past is on its way somewhere. If a bee does sting, it hurts honestly and briefly: tell a grown-up, wash the spot, and a cold cloth takes the heat down. A very swollen face or any trouble breathing means a grown-up calls a doctor straight away.'},
'snail':{
 title:'Snail',one:'A slow walker with its house on its back — meet the garden animal your child can truly out-watch.',
 seoTitle:'Snail Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach Snail Watching (Class 31)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: why a snail carries its shell, how slime lets it glide and stick, and an after-rain hunt that makes slow fascinating.',
 intro:'This lesson makes the slowest animal in the garden the best first subject: a snail moves at exactly the speed a four-year-old studies, and everything about it — shell, slime, feelers — is visible without any chasing.',
 what:'A snail is a soft-bodied animal with a spiral shell it never leaves: the shell is its home, its shield and its bones in one. Underneath, a single muscular foot ripples forward, laying down slippery slime as it goes.',
 mean:'The shell answers a big question — where does a soft animal keep safe? — by carrying the answer along. The slime is not mess; it is a road, a glue and a drying-guard all at once, which is why a snail can climb a wall or slide under a leaf and stick there.',
 know:'Snails come out when it is damp — after rain, at dawn, in shady corners — because drying out is their biggest danger. The shell grows with the snail, coil by coil, and the two feelers on top carry tiny eyes at their tips.',
 why:'Snails reward patience like nothing else in the garden: the child who sits still for two minutes gets a whole show — stretching, gliding, tasting the air. Slow animals teach the watching skills fast animals cannot.',
 dev:'Sustained attention (snails reward stillness), close observation, gentle handling of ideas like protection and shelter, and the patience-muscle that underpins every later science lesson.',
 vocab:'snail, shell, slime, slow, feelers, damp',
 teach:'1) Show the card: who is watching whom, and what is the snail wearing on its back? Name the home-carrying trick.\n2) Ask the big question: where does a snail sleep? Answer — inside itself, door closed. The shell is bedroom and shield together.\n3) Hunt after rain: shady grass, damp walls, under pots. Move slowly; slow animals trust slow people.\n4) Follow the shiny trail backwards and ask where the snail came from — slime is a road you can read.\n5) Finish by watching one snail stretch out and glide: two quiet minutes is a long time at four, and a snail makes every second count.',
 say:'"A snail carries its house everywhere — no other animal does that. When it is scared, it pulls everything inside and shuts the door. Underneath is one strong foot that ripples like a wave, and the slime is its slippery road so the soft parts never get hurt on the ground."',
 notice:'Ask your child: what shape is the snail\u2019s shell — can you trace the spiral with a finger in the air? What is the child in the picture doing? Where do you think the snail is heading? Can you find anything else in the picture that looks damp?',
 ask:'"Where does a snail keep its house?" "Why do we find snails after rain?" "What is the shiny line on the ground?" "What does a snail do when it is scared?"',
 childSay:'"The snail took its house inside!" "Look — its slime road goes all the way to the wall!" Your child may crawl slowly around the garden calling themselves a snail, or check the same wall every morning for "their" snail.',
 real:'Snail shells show up in stories, spirals and staircases once your child starts looking — the swirl is one of nature\u2019s favourite shapes. Gardeners have a different view of snails: your child now understands both sides, the admirer and the lettuce-farmer.',
 play:'Spiral hunt: draw one big spiral, then find snail-swirl shapes around you — shells, cinnamon rolls, curled fern tips, the coil of a hose. Then slow-race: everyone moves like a snail from sofa to door; last one wins, of course.',
 check:'Ask: "What does a snail carry on its back? What is the shiny trail for? When do snails come out?" Shell-home, slime-road, after-rain = landed.',
 tips:'If your child wants to hold a snail, wet hands first and keep it low over soft grass — a dropped shell cracks. Always put the snail back exactly where it was found; its whole world is that one damp corner.'},
'spider':{
 title:'Spider',one:'Eight legs, one clever net — how a spider builds a trap that catches dinner all by itself.',
 seoTitle:'Spider Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach Spider Webs (Class 31)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how a web works as trap and dinner bell, why spiders have eight legs and are not insects, and a dewy-morning web hunt.',
 intro:'This lesson turns spider webs from spooky into clever: a net designed by an animal, strung between plants, that catches the spider\u2019s dinner without one single chase. Fear shrinks fast when engineering takes its place.',
 what:'A spider web is a net made of silk — a thread the spider\u2019s own body spins. The sticky spirals catch flying insects; the spider feels the shake through its legs and knows dinner has landed.',
 mean:'The web is the spider\u2019s fishing net and dinner bell at once: it catches food AND rings the news. Silk comes from the spider\u2019s own body, thread by thread, which makes a web one of the few tools an animal builds from itself.',
 know:'Spiders have eight legs, so they are arachnids — not insects, which have six. Silk is astonishingly strong for its weight, and some spiders rebuild the whole trap daily — eating the old one first to recycle the material.',
 why:'Webs make engineering visible: spokes, frame, spiral — a child can see the design. Learning that spiders eat pests (flies, mosquitoes) flips the fear script: the web is a helpful trap, and the spider is night-shift pest control.',
 dev:'Respectful distance from small creatures, design thinking (parts with jobs: frame, spokes, trap), the insect-versus-arachnid sorting idea, and courage that grows from understanding rather than avoidance.',
 vocab:'spider, web, silk, legs, sticky, catch',
 teach:'1) Show the card: who is sitting in the middle of the net, and what is the child looking at? Count the spider\u2019s legs together — how many?\n2) Explain the plan of a web: strong spokes first, like a bicycle wheel, then the sticky spiral wound round and round.\n3) Hunt for a web in the early morning or after rain, when dew drops make every thread shine. Look with eyes only — the web took hours to build.\n4) Sort it out loud: spiders have eight legs, flies and bees six. Eight means arachnid, and arachnids spin silk.\n5) Close with the good-deed idea: the fly a web catches is one less fly in the kitchen. The spider is the garden\u2019s quiet cleaner.',
 say:'"A spider makes its own thread — real silk, from its own body. It builds the spokes first, then a spiral sticky enough that flying insects get stuck. When something lands, the web shakes like a doorbell, and the spider comes out to collect its dinner."',
 notice:'Ask your child: where is the spider sitting? What is the child in the picture doing — touching the web or looking? Can you see the lines of the web crossing like a wheel? How many legs can you count on the spider?',
 ask:'"What is a web made of?" "How many legs does a spider have?" "What happens when a fly lands in the web?" "Are we allowed to touch a web? Why not?"',
 childSay:'"The web is the spider\u2019s dinner bell!" "Eight legs — arachnid, not insect!" Your child may hunt for webs every dewy morning, or gently warn guests that the garden web took the spider all night to build, so hands off.',
 real:'Webs appear where food flies: window corners, garden gates, hedge gaps, bicycle frames left overnight. Once your child can name the wheel pattern, a walk becomes a web safari — and empty webs are evidence the builder is nearby.',
 play:'Be the spider: tape or string a "web" between chair legs, then throw paper-fly balls at it — which stick, which fall through? The sticky-zone idea lands hard. Then your child repairs the broken web with new string, feeling how fiddly silk work is.',
 check:'Ask: "What do spiders build webs from? How many legs? What happens when food lands?" Silk, eight, the web shakes and the spider comes = landed.',
 tips:'Dew or drizzle is the web-hunter\u2019s friend; midday sun hides the threads. If a web is built across a doorframe, leave it one day and watch the spider repair it — better than any worksheet on engineering.'},
'butterfly-caterpillar':{
 title:'Butterfly Begins as a Caterpillar',one:'Before the wings, the appetite — meet the caterpillar whose whole first job is eating and growing.',
 seoTitle:'Butterfly Begins as a Caterpillar Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach Caterpillars (Class 31)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what a caterpillar is doing on that leaf, why eating is its whole job, and a kind hunt that trains gentle looking.',
 intro:'This lesson starts the butterfly story at the beginning — a tiny egg on a leaf hatches into a hungry crawler — and gives your child the garden habit that matters: find a caterpillar, look closely, and leave it to its lunch.',
 what:'A caterpillar is a butterfly\u2019s baby stage: it hatches from an egg laid on a leaf and spends its days eating leaves and growing, shedding its tight skin several times before it is ready for the next stage.',
 mean:'The caterpillar\u2019s whole job is one word: grow. Every bite is stored for the big change ahead. That is why it never stops munching — it is a living lunchbox, packing away fuel for wings it does not have yet.',
 know:'Butterfly mothers lay eggs on the exact leaves their babies can eat. A caterpillar\u2019s body is soft with rings called segments, and it grows so fast it must crawl out of its own skin again and again.',
 why:'Knowing what caterpillars are DOING on leaves changes how a child treats them: not a toy to grab but a worker on a deadline. It also sets up the life-cycle idea — this card is chapter one of a story your child will meet again.',
 dev:'Gentle observation, impulse control (look, do not grab), the idea that animals have jobs at each stage of life, and story-sequencing instincts — beginnings, middles and endings.',
 vocab:'caterpillar, leaf, egg, eat, grow, butterfly',
 teach:'1) Show the card: what is resting beside the leaf, and what is the child watching? Name both — butterfly AND caterpillar — and ask how they could be connected.\n2) Tell the beginning: a butterfly laid a tiny egg on a leaf, and out crawled this caterpillar. Chapter one of a story.\n3) Explain the job: caterpillars eat and eat and eat — leaves are their only food, and growing is their only work.\n4) Hunt gently: look along and under garden leaves for nibbled holes and slow crawlers. Found one? Count its body rings, watch it munch if you are lucky — then leave it exactly there.\n5) End with the promise: this crawler is packing away fuel for wings. Chapter two comes next lesson.',
 say:'"Every butterfly was a caterpillar first. The mother laid her egg on just the right leaf, and the baby\u2019s job is simple: eat leaves, grow bigger, shed your skin when it gets tight. All that food is being packed away for the wings it will grow later."',
 notice:'Ask your child: what is the child in the picture watching? Can you find the caterpillar AND the butterfly? Which one has wings? Which one has more legs? Where do you think the caterpillar is hiding on the leaf?',
 ask:'"What was the butterfly before it had wings?" "What does a caterpillar eat all day?" "Why does the mother lay her egg on a leaf?" "What should we do if we find a caterpillar?"',
 childSay:'"Caterpillars eat and eat and eat!" "Do not grab it — it is growing!" Your child may check the same leaves on every garden walk, or retell the story backwards: "the butterfly\u2019s mummy was an egg, no wait — the egg was the butterfly\u2019s beginning!"',
 real:'Cabbage patches, nettles and hedge rows are caterpillar nurseries once you know to look for nibbled edges — those holes are breakfast evidence. Farmers know it too: caterpillars eat crops, birds eat caterpillars, and the garden keeps its balance.',
 play:'Munch game: cut a big green paper leaf, give your child a hole punch, and let them "eat" it like a caterpillar — punch, punch, punch. Then measure: how many punches until the caterpillar is full? Body-ring craft follows with a painted egg box.',
 check:'Ask: "What is a caterpillar\u2019s job? What does it eat? What do we do when we find one?" Eat-and-grow, leaves, look-and-leave = landed.',
 tips:'Caterpillar hunting teaches more with a rule of two hands behind the back — eyes do the touching. If your child begs to keep one, a leaf from the same plant in a jar with air holes is a short, honest visit; release it the same day.'},
'butterfly-life-cycle':{
 title:'Butterfly Life Cycle',one:'Four stages, one animal — egg, caterpillar, chrysalis, butterfly: the biggest change in the garden.',
 seoTitle:'Butterfly Life Cycle Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach Metamorphosis (Class 31)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: the four butterfly stages in order, what really happens inside a chrysalis, and a draw-the-wheel activity to retell it.',
 intro:'This lesson takes the whole journey at once — egg, caterpillar, chrysalis, butterfly — and teaches it as a four-chapter story your child can sequence, draw and retell, ending with the word that names the magic: metamorphosis.',
 what:'A butterfly changes form four times in one life: egg, then caterpillar, then chrysalis, then winged adult. Inside the chrysalis, the old caterpillar body dissolves and rebuilds as something with wings.',
 mean:'Metamorphosis means "changing shape" — and the chrysalis is where the quiet work happens: nothing is wasted, the old body is rearranged into a new one. Change in nature is not instant; it is staged, and every stage has a job.',
 know:'The chrysalis looks still but is working hard inside — scientists who peeked found the caterpillar turns almost to liquid before rebuilding. A hatched butterfly\u2019s wings start crumpled and must fill and dry before the first flight.',
 why:'This is the most dramatic change your child can actually find in a garden, which makes it the perfect first life cycle. Sequencing the four stages builds ordering skills, and the story carries a quiet message: big change is natural, and slow stages count too.',
 dev:'Sequencing and retelling (four chapters in order), vocabulary building (a real science word), comfort with transformation stories — tadpoles and seeds will reuse the pattern — and drawing as a thinking tool.',
 vocab:'egg, caterpillar, chrysalis, butterfly, change, wings',
 teach:'1) Show the card and hunt the three characters on the leaf: caterpillar, chrysalis, butterfly. Which one is missing from the picture? The egg — that is chapter one.\n2) Walk the story with one finger: first the egg on the leaf, then the munching caterpillar, then the quiet chrysalis, then the wings.\n3) Tell the chrysalis secret: it looks like nothing is happening, but inside, the caterpillar is rebuilding its whole body into a butterfly.\n4) Draw the wheel: four circles on paper, one for each stage — your child fills them and then retells the story round and round.\n5) Learn the big word: metamorphosis. Clap it out — me-ta-mor-fo-sis — and let your child own it.',
 say:'"A butterfly has four chapters. Egg, tiny and round on a leaf. Caterpillar, eating everything. Chrysalis, hanging like a sleeping bag while its body rebuilds inside. Then butterfly — new wings, new life. The word for that big change is metamorphosis."',
 notice:'Ask your child: how many stages can you find on the leaf in the picture? Which stage is the caterpillar, and which is the chrysalis? Does the butterfly look new or old? What is the child watching for?',
 ask:'"What are the four stages, in order?" "What happens inside the chrysalis?" "What is the big word for changing shape?" "Which stage do you think takes longest?"',
 childSay:'"Me-ta-mor-fo-sis!" — said at full volume, often. "The chrysalis is a sleeping bag, not a shell." Your child may line up four toys to show the four stages, or retell the wheel starting anywhere and insist it still works.',
 real:'The pattern repeats all over nature once your child knows to look: tadpoles become frogs, seeds become plants, and mealworms in a pet shop turn into beetles. Same shape of story — start small, change completely — in garden after garden.',
 play:'Stage race: four corners of the room — egg (curled tiny), caterpillar (wriggle-crawl), chrysalis (stand still, arms wrapped), butterfly (flap and fly). Call the stages in any order and let your child transform on the spot. Then backwards!',
 check:'Ask: "Name the four stages in order. What happens inside the chrysalis? What is metamorphosis?" Egg-caterpillar-chrysalis-butterfly, rebuilding, change = landed.',
 tips:'Let your child draw the wheel BEFORE hearing the story and guess what happens between stages — the wrong guesses ("the caterpillar takes a nap") make the truth land harder. Go chrysalis-hunting under leaves and along fence rails; they hide in plain sight, looking like tiny dried leaves.'},
'frog':{
 title:'Frog',one:'A swimmer that grows into a hopper — follow the frog from jelly-dot egg to jumping pond champion.',
 seoTitle:'Frog Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach Tadpoles (Class 31)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how a tadpole turns into a hopping frog, what gills and lungs have to do with it, and a spring pond watch.',
 intro:'This lesson follows the second great change-your-body story in the garden: the frog. Your child learns that pond animals start as swimmers, watches what legs are for, and practises the pondside habit of looking without scooping.',
 what:'A frog starts life as an egg in a blob of jelly, hatches as a tadpole that swims and breathes underwater, then slowly grows four legs, loses its tail and hops out onto land as a breathing, jumping frog.',
 mean:'The tadpole is not a different animal from the frog — it is the same frog at a different age, built for a different home. First a water body with a tail and gills, then a land body with legs and lungs: one life, two designs.',
 know:'Tadpoles hatch in spring from frogspawn — soft jelly dots laid on pond plants. As a tadpole grows, its tail gets shorter until it disappears, and lungs take over from gills. Frogs breathe a little through their skin too, and they hop instead of walk.',
 why:'Frogs give your child a second life-cycle after the butterfly, and this one happens in a place they can visit: a pond. Matching bodies to homes — tail for swimming, legs for hopping — is the first step toward understanding habitats.',
 dev:'Sequencing a life story in order, comparing bodies across stages, the habitat idea (water animal, land animal), and gentle pondside discipline: watching without dipping hands or nets into the water.',
 vocab:'frog, tadpole, tail, legs, pond, jump',
 teach:'1) Show the card: who is watching whom, and where is the frog sitting? Name the animal and its home.\n2) Tell the beginning: a frog starts as a jelly dot in the pond, then wiggles out as a tadpole — a tiny swimmer with a tail and no legs.\n3) Walk the change: back legs sprout first, then front legs, then the tail shrinks away while lungs grow for breathing air.\n4) Ask what legs can do that a tail cannot — then hop like frogs across the room, crouch and jump, crouch and jump.\n5) Close with the pond rule: frogspawn and tadpoles stay in their pond — we look, we count, and we leave them to grow.',
 say:'"A baby frog cannot hop at first — it swims! It hatches from a jelly egg as a tadpole, with a long tail for paddling. Little by little it grows legs, the tail disappears, and one day it climbs out of the water and jumps. Same frog, brand-new body."',
 notice:'Ask your child: who is the child watching? Where is the frog sitting — can you find the lily pad? What is the frog doing? Is the child close to the water or keeping a little distance?',
 ask:'"What does a baby frog look like before it has legs?" "What happens to the tadpole\u2019s tail?" "Why does a frog need legs?" "What do we do at a pond — scoop or watch?"',
 childSay:'"The tadpole sucked its tail back in!" "Frogs drink through their skin — that is so weird!" (true, and your child said it first). Your child may frog-hop everywhere for a week, or report every spring that the pond frogs will be laying jelly eggs again.',
 real:'Frogs turn up wherever water sits still long enough: garden ponds, ditches, rain barrels. Once your child knows the tadpole story, a spring pond visit becomes a scavenger hunt — jelly eggs one week, wiggling tadpoles the next, tiny froglets by summer.',
 play:'Tadpole-to-frog transformation race: start curled small like an egg, wiggle-swim across the rug, grow "legs" (arms out), then hop the last stretch to a lily pad cushion. Call out the stages and let your child transform on command.',
 check:'Ask: "What does a tadpole use to swim? What grows for hopping? What do we do at the pond?" Tail, legs, watch-only = landed.',
 tips:'Pond dipping needs grown-up hands in charge; bring a white tub to scoop-and-release only where the site allows it, and return every creature straight away. A good pond book or zoomed-in photos stretch the lesson on rainy days.',
 safety:'Ponds earn real respect: little scientists stay a grown-up arm\u2019s length from the water, sit down to watch, and frogspawn or tadpoles never come home — they are living animals, not souvenirs. Nets and buckets only when a grown-up runs the dipping.'},
'fish':{
 title:'Fish',one:'A breather with no lungs — how a goldfish grabs air from water through its gills, door-opening and all.',
 seoTitle:'Fish Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach Gills (Class 31)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how gills pull air out of water, what fins and tails do, and a count-the-gill-beats watch at any aquarium or bowl.',
 intro:'This lesson answers the question every child asks at the fish tank — how on earth does it breathe down there? — and turns a goldfish bowl into a breathing laboratory your child can count, compare and imitate.',
 what:'A fish breathes with gills: water goes in the mouth, pours over the gills hidden behind the gill covers, and the gills snatch the oxygen out of the water — so a fish never has to surface for air the way we do.',
 mean:'We solve the breathing problem with lungs that take air from the sky; fish solve it with gills that take air from the water. Same job, different tools — comparing solutions is what scientists do all their lives.',
 know:'Fish never stop pumping: the gill covers flap open and shut like tiny doors, all day and night. Fins steer and brake, the flat tail is the engine, and most fish rest without ever closing their eyes — they have no eyelids to close.',
 why:'Gills open up the compare-and-contrast habit: fish, frogs and people all need oxygen, but each body finds it somewhere different. Your child also learns to read animal bodies as equipment — every part has a job.',
 dev:'Observation of small repeated movements (gill beats), counting practice, comparison across animals (gills versus lungs), and the deep idea that bodies are built to match where they live.',
 vocab:'fish, gills, water, breathe, fins, tail',
 teach:'1) Show the card: what is the child peering at, and where does the goldfish live? Name the bowl and the fish.\n2) Ask the big question: our lungs grab air out of the sky — how does a fish grab air out of the water? Let guesses fly before you answer.\n3) Explain the gill doors: water swims in the mouth, washes over the gills, and the gills grab the air hidden in the water.\n4) Watch a real fish if you can — a bowl, a tank, an aquarium — and count gill beats together for ten seconds.\n5) Compare: you breathe about twenty times a minute at rest; was the fish faster or slower? Finish with fins for steering and the tail for pushing.',
 say:'"A fish carries its own breathing gear inside its head. It takes a big sip of water, and while the water pours out over its gills, the gills grab the air out of it. Those flaps opening and closing are the fish breathing — it never has to come up for air."',
 notice:'Ask your child: what is the child peering at? Can you find the goldfish? Is the fish swimming in a bowl or the sea? What do you think the fish is looking at?',
 ask:'"What does a fish use to breathe?" "Do fish breathe air or water?" "What do fins do — and the tail?" "How long can you hold your breath under water?"',
 childSay:'"Gills are doors that grab air!" Your child may puff cheeks and pretend to be a fish, flap hands by their neck like gills, or count the gill beats of every fish they ever meet — bowls, aquariums, and the fish counter at the market.',
 real:'Fish live in every patch of water bigger than a bucket: bowls, tanks, garden ponds, rivers, the sea. Once gills make sense, the aquarium becomes a breathing lesson on repeat — and your child knows why a fish out of water flops: its gear only works wet.',
 play:'Gill-breathing pretend: scoop "water" with big armfuls, clap hands to your neck and whisper "grab!" each time — once, twice, all day long. Then switch: you are the fish, your child counts your gill beats, and you race to twenty.',
 check:'Ask: "What does a fish breathe with? What do gills grab from the water? What pushes a fish along?" Gills, oxygen, the tail = landed.',
 tips:'A home goldfish bowl is a live experiment: one quiet minute of sitting still, and the gill counting works best when nobody taps the glass. Feeding time is bonus science — fish rush the surface, then settle back into their gill rhythm.'},
'bones':{
 title:'Bones',one:'The frame under your skin — find five hard places and meet the skeleton that holds you up all day.',
 seoTitle:'Bones Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach the Skeleton (Class 31)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what the skeleton does, why babies start with more bones than grown-ups, and a find-five-bones touch hunt to play today.',
 intro:'This lesson makes the invisible inside visible: your child presses, bends and wiggles their way to discovering the frame under their skin — and learns that the same quiet scaffolding is busy making blood all day long.',
 what:'Bones are the hard parts inside your body: 206 of them in a grown-up, joined at joints. The skeleton holds you upright, guards the soft organs, gives muscles something to pull on — and the biggest bones are blood factories.',
 mean:'A body without bones would be a puddle: bones are why you stand, sit, jump and carry. Joints are where the design gets clever — the elbow bends one way, the shoulder swings a circle, and every bend has a limit that keeps it safe.',
 know:'A baby starts with about 300 soft bones; as you grow, some join together, leaving 206. Bones grow with you — the skeleton of a four-year-old is halfway to its final size. Blood cells are made deep inside the biggest bones, like a factory under the floor.',
 why:'Feeling your own skeleton turns "bones" from a Halloween word into a body fact. It builds self-knowledge, safety sense (helmets and elbows exist because bones can break), and the habit of asking what is inside things.',
 dev:'Body awareness and self-care vocabulary, sorting (bendy joints versus straight bones), counting to five on the touch hunt, and early engineering thinking: frames, supports and joints, just like a crane or a doll\u2019s stand.',
 vocab:'bones, skeleton, joints, bend, ribs, skull',
 teach:'1) Show the card: who is standing beside the child, and does the skeleton look friendly? Wave to the skeleton together.\n2) Start the feel-hunt: press gently on the shin — that hard bit is a bone saying hello. Find the ribs, the elbow, the knee, the skull.\n3) Bend test: wiggle fingers, bend the elbow, swing a shoulder — bones are straight but joints are the bendy hinges between them.\n4) Tell the factory secret: inside the biggest bones, blood is being made all day, every day, without a break.\n5) Count up: a baby starts with about 300 bones, and some join together as you grow — a grown-up has 206. Your child is a building site, in the best way.',
 say:'"Under your skin there is a whole frame, like the poles inside a tent. Bones hold you up, protect the soft parts, and make your blood inside the big ones. Feel that hard ridge in your shin — that is your skeleton, saying hello from inside."',
 notice:'Ask your child: who is standing next to the child in the picture? Does the skeleton look friendly or grumpy? Can you find the skeleton\u2019s ribs? Where are your own ribs right now?',
 ask:'"What do bones do for your body?" "Where do two bones meet?" "What is made inside your biggest bones?" "Why does a baby have more bones than you?"',
 childSay:'"I can feel my skeleton — it is everywhere!" "Bones make my blood!" Your child may press knuckles to find hard bits all day, count the bones in one hand, or explain that babies have "more bones but smaller ones, and some zip together".',
 real:'Skeletons are hiding in every meal and playtime: chicken bones on a plate, the hard knobs of your ankles on stairs, X-ray pictures at the doctor. Once your child can find five bones, every bump and bend becomes a map of the frame inside.',
 play:'Skeleton dance: call out a bone — "ribs!", "skull!", "shin!" — and your child pats it fast, then freezes like a statue. Slow-mo round next: every move in slow motion, feeling which parts bend at joints and which stay straight.',
 check:'Ask: "What holds your body up? Where do bones bend? What is made inside big bones?" Skeleton, joints, blood = landed.',
 tips:'Keep the touch hunt gentle — bones to find, not bones to bang. If a cast or an X-ray shows up in your family or a friend, bring it out: a real broken-bone story teaches more about why bones matter than any diagram.'},
'eyes':{
 title:'Eyes',one:'Cameras you carry everywhere — how eyes catch light, why we blink, and what happens when hearing takes over.',
 seoTitle:'Eyes Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach Seeing (Class 31)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how eyes turn light into pictures, why blinking is a tiny wash, and a blink-count and ears-only game to play today.',
 intro:'This lesson treats sight as the superpower it is: your child learns what eyes actually catch, plays the blink game, then switches senses off and on — and discovers that closing the eyes turns the volume up on everything else.',
 what:'Eyes are light catchers: light bounces off everything around you, and the front of your eye gathers it in like a little window, turning the bouncing light into a picture your brain can read. Eyelids blink to wash and protect, and the pair team up on every image.',
 mean:'Sight is your child\u2019s busiest sense, and it runs on teamwork: two eyes, each catching a slightly different picture, and the brain merging them into one. Blinking is maintenance — thousands of tiny window-wipes a day, mostly without you noticing.',
 know:'You blink about fifteen times a minute — that adds up to thousands of washes a day. Tears are the eye-cleaning crew, called out for dust or big feelings. Eyes need sleep, hate pokes, and if the world goes blurry, a grown-up and an eye doctor should hear about it.',
 why:'Knowing how eyes work makes the other senses louder: shut the eyes and hearing, touch and smell step forward. It also plants eye-care habits — no rubbing with dirty hands, reporting blurry vision — through science, not just rules.',
 dev:'Sensory awareness (sight versus hearing), self-care routines, the teamwork idea (two eyes, one picture), and gentle experiment discipline: trying not to blink, timing, and noticing what changes when one sense closes.',
 vocab:'eyes, see, blink, light, eyelids, tears',
 teach:'1) Show the card: what tool is the child holding up, and what is it pointed at? Name it — a magnifying glass, and an enormous eye.\n2) Explain the catching: light bounces off everything — your toy, the table, your face — and your eyes catch the bounce and turn it into pictures.\n3) Play blink-count: try not to blink for as long as you can (it is hard!), then blink fast ten times. That wetting is each blink\u2019s job.\n4) Switch senses: close the eyes for ten seconds and listen. What got louder? The ears take over when the eyes clock off.\n5) End with care: eyes sleep, eyes wash themselves with tears, and pokes or blur mean a grown-up needs to know.',
 say:'"Your eyes are little light catchers. Light bounces off everything and jumps into your eyes, and your brain turns it into pictures — that is seeing. Every blink is a tiny wipe to keep the window clean, and both eyes work together like two cameras making one film."',
 notice:'Ask your child: what is the child holding up? What is the magnifying glass pointed at? How big does the eye look — bigger than a fist? Is the eye looking back at you?',
 ask:'"What do your eyes catch to make pictures?" "Why do we blink?" "What got louder when you closed your eyes?" "What should we do if things look blurry?"',
 childSay:'"I blinked two hundred times today!" (close — thousands). "My ears got louder when my eyes went to sleep!" Your child may play eyes-closed detective around the house, or report every blink as a "window wash" for a week.',
 real:'Eyes work everywhere light works: reading pictures, catching a ball, spotting a ladybird on a leaf. Dim rooms show the limits — eyes need light to catch — and sunglasses on a bright beach, washed hands away from eyes, and good sleep are the care package in action.',
 play:'Ears-only safari: your child closes their eyes while you move around the room making small sounds — keys, a page, a whisper — and they point to where each sound came from. Then swap roles. Closing the eyes is how the other senses get their turn to shine.',
 check:'Ask: "What do eyes catch to make pictures? What does a blink do? Which sense gets louder with eyes closed?" Light, washing, hearing = landed.',
 tips:'Keep the eyes-closed games short and willing — three to ten seconds feels like a long time at four. If your child squints at books or sits very close to screens, mention it to a grown-up eye doctor; catching it early is the whole game.'},
'heart':{
 title:'Heart',one:'A pump the size of your fist — feel it thump after a jump and meet the muscle that never clocks off.',
 seoTitle:'Heart Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach the Heartbeat (Class 31)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what the heart pumps and why running makes it race, plus a jump-and-feel experiment and a rest-and-compare chart to try.',
 intro:'This lesson makes the busiest organ in the body audible: ten jumps, a palm on the chest, and your child can feel their own engine speed up — then learn what the thump-thump is actually delivering, and to whom.',
 what:'The heart is a muscle that squeezes again and again, pushing blood all around the body. Blood is the delivery crew: it carries oxygen and food to working muscles and picks up what they are done with, and the heartbeat is the sound of that pump at work.',
 mean:'Move fast and your muscles shout for more oxygen, so the heart pumps faster to keep up — that is why running makes your chest drum. Rest, and the beat settles down. The heart is answering demand, all day, every day, no weekends.',
 know:'Your heart is about as big as your fist and beats roughly a hundred thousand times a day. Exercise trains it — a well-run heart is strong at pushing. A toy stethoscope, or a grown-up\u2019s ear pressed to the chest, hears the pump directly.',
 why:'Feeling the heartbeat links exercise to the body\u2019s answer, which is the first cause-and-effect your child can hear with their own hand. It also builds the lifelong idea that moving your body is how you train it — hearts love to be asked to work.',
 dev:'Body awareness, cause-and-effect reasoning (run, then faster beat), measuring by feel (fast, faster, resting), and turn-taking games that end in stillness — the rest-and-listen part is half the lesson.',
 vocab:'heart, blood, pump, beat, fast, rest',
 teach:'1) Show the card: what is the child listening to, and what are they holding to their ears? Name the tool — a stethoscope.\n2) Feel it first: hand flat on the chest, find the thump-thump. That is the heart, pumping right now.\n3) Run the experiment: jump ten times, hand on chest, feel again — faster! Rest until the beat calms, jump again, compare the two.\n4) Explain the deliveries: blood is the van that hauls air and food out to your toes, your fingers, your brain — and the heart is the engine driving it round.\n5) Close with the fist: a heart is about fist-sized, and it squeezes day and night — every thump is one delivery run.',
 say:'"Your heart is a pump hidden in your chest. Every squeeze pushes blood out on a delivery run — carrying air and food to your toes, your fingers, your brain. When you jump, your muscles ask for more, so the pump speeds up. That drumming you feel? Your engine, working hard."',
 notice:'Ask your child: what is the child listening to? What are they holding against their ears? What color is the heart? Can you point to where your own heart is hiding?',
 ask:'"What does the heart push around your body?" "What happens to your heartbeat when you run?" "What makes the beat slow down again?" "How big is your heart?"',
 childSay:'"My heart goes fast-fast-fast when I jump!" "It slowed down — my engine is resting." Your child may demand jump-and-listen rounds every evening, press a toy stethoscope to everyone\u2019s chest, or announce "my heart is delivering!" on the stairs.',
 real:'Hearts speed up for stairs, dancing, giggling fits and big feelings — your child now has a name for that drumming chest. Calm-down moments get scientific too: slow breaths, a quiet cuddle, and feeling the beat settle are the rest half of the experiment.',
 play:'Heartbeat drums: pat your own chest to your heart\u2019s rhythm while your child copies, then jump ten times together and drum fast. End with a slow-lion-breath round until both drums rest. Loud, fast, slow, quiet — the whole lesson played on your own chest.',
 check:'Ask: "What does the heart pump? Why does it beat faster when you run? What is blood carrying?" Blood, more oxygen asked for, air and food = landed.',
 tips:'A real stethoscope or a cheap toy one turns this into a favourite toy for weeks — listen to hearts, but also to tummy rumbles and breathing. If your child feels fluttery heartbeats when scared, the science helps: that is the pump getting ready, not a problem.'},
'teeth':{
 title:'Teeth',one:'Slice, tear, grind — bite like a rabbit, chew like a lion and meet the three tool-kinds in your mouth.',
 seoTitle:'Teeth Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach Tooth Jobs (Class 31)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: the three tooth jobs at dinner, why the tools need brushing, and a mirror game that turns every meal into anatomy.',
 intro:'This lesson turns the dinner table into a tool bench: your child discovers which teeth slice, which tear and which grind — and why tools that work all day deserve a proper wash, twice a day, back teeth included.',
 what:'Teeth are food tools in three shapes: flat front teeth slice off bites, pointy side teeth tear tougher food, and wide back teeth grind everything to a mash your tummy can handle. Brushing and spit keep the tools clean between jobs.',
 mean:'Different shapes do different jobs — the same idea that sorts scissors from saws from spoons. Your child can feel it at every meal: the apple-slice crunch at the front, the big grinding work at the back where nobody can see.',
 know:'You grow just two sets of teeth in one life: the baby set now, and a hidden grown-up set waiting in the jaw like seeds under soil. Wobbly teeth are normal — the baby tooth is making room. The back grinders matter most and hide from brushes, so they need the longest scrub.',
 why:'Tooth science makes brushing meaningful instead of a rule from the sky: the tools need cleaning because food sticks to them. Knowing the three jobs also turns eating into an experiment — which teeth am I using right now? — that plays out at every single meal.',
 dev:'Sorting by shape and job, body-part vocabulary, the two-sets idea (a first version of life stages), and habit-building: brushing becomes tool maintenance, with the hidden back teeth as the priority target.',
 vocab:'teeth, bite, chew, brush, front, grind',
 teach:'1) Show the card: what is the child doing, and how big are those teeth? Is the brushing child smiling? Name the job — tool washing.\n2) Set the mirror mission: bite like a rabbit with the front teeth — feel them slicing? Then chew like a lion at the back — feel the grinding?\n3) Sort dinner out loud: bread sliced by the front, chicken torn by the pointy ones, rice ground by the big back teeth.\n4) Count in the mirror: how many teeth can you see? Then hunt the sneaky back ones — the grinders hide from brushes, so they get the longest scrub.\n5) Tell the secret: a second set of grown-up teeth waits in the jaw below, and these baby teeth are the first team — keep them clean so they hand over well.',
 say:'"Your mouth is a toolbox. The flat ones at the front slice like little knives, the pointy ones at the sides tear, and the wide ones at the back grind everything small. Tools that work all day need a wash — that is why we brush, especially the shy grinders hiding at the back."',
 notice:'Ask your child: what is the child doing? Are the teeth being brushed big or small? Is the child smiling while brushing? Where are your own back teeth hiding right now?',
 ask:'"Which teeth slice off a bite of apple?" "What do the wide back teeth do?" "Why do teeth need brushing?" "What is waiting under your baby teeth?"',
 childSay:'"I am grinding like a horse!" "The back teeth are hiding from the brush — I got them!" Your child may bite apples only with the front teeth for a while, narrate every meal by tooth job, or announce that the tooth brush is a "tool wash".',
 real:'Every meal is a tooth lesson: sandwiches sliced, pizza torn, apples crunched at the front. Dentist visits upgrade the science — the mirror, the counted teeth, the polishing — and a lost first tooth becomes the proudest show-and-tell of the year.',
 play:'Tool tester dinner: serve three foods — something to slice (apple), something to tear (bread roll), something to grind (rice) — and let your child announce which teeth take each job. Brush a big paper tooth afterwards with a dry brush to finish the maintenance story.',
 check:'Ask: "What do front teeth do? What do back teeth do? Why do we brush?" Slice, grind, wash the tools = landed.',
 tips:'Two-minute brushing works best with a song or a mirror — the back teeth are the slow zone, so park there. If a tooth goes wobbly, celebrate early: a child who knows the two-sets story treats the wiggle as news, not a worry.'},
'plant':{
 title:'What Plants Need',one:'Three things and a plant is happy — water, light and soil, with a two-jar experiment to prove it.',
 seoTitle:'What Plants Need Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach Plant Needs (Class 31)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: the three things every plant runs on, and the grow-two-beans, hide-one-in-the-cupboard experiment that teaches fair tests.',
 intro:'This lesson sets up the classic first experiment in all of science: grow two plants the same, take something away from one, and watch. Your child learns the three plant needs — and how to test fairly by changing only one thing.',
 what:'Plants run on three things: water sipped up by the roots, light caught by the leaves to cook food, and soil to anchor in and feed from. Miss one out and the plant tells you — drooping, pale or stalling.',
 mean:'The leaves are the kitchen, the roots are the straws, the soil is the pantry and the anchor. A plant cannot walk to its dinner — it builds its food from light, water and air right where it stands, which is why taking any ingredient away shows fast.',
 know:'A plant left in darkness turns pale and leggy, stretching after light it cannot find. Roots drink even when the leaves look dry, and overwatering drowns them — plants can have too much of a good thing. Different plants like different amounts.',
 why:'This is where fair testing begins: two jars, watered the same, one in the light and one in the dark — change one thing, watch, conclude. Your child meets the controlled experiment in bean form, and it will carry through every science lesson after.',
 dev:'Fair-test thinking (change one thing), daily observation and comparison, responsibility through watering routines, and vocabulary for plant parts that recurs through seed, tree and garden lessons.',
 vocab:'plant, water, light, soil, roots, leaves',
 teach:'1) Show the card: what is the child giving the seedling? Can you find the roots down in the soil? Name the three needs — water, light, soil.\n2) Ask the detective question: what would a plant do with no water? With no light? With no soil? Collect predictions before testing.\n3) Plant the test: two bean jars on the windowsill, watered the same — then one jar goes into a dark cupboard. Only ONE thing changed.\n4) Check daily: both jars get water, but only one gets light. Which bean is green and sturdy, which is pale and stretchy?\n5) Let your child deliver the verdict: plants need light. Then run it again with water withheld if they are hooked — scientists love a repeat.',
 say:'"A plant is stuck in one spot, so it carries everything it needs around with it: roots that sip water from the soil, and leaves that catch sunlight and cook it into plant food. Take away one ingredient and the whole machine slows down — that is what our dark-cupboard bean is showing us."',
 notice:'Ask your child: what is the child giving the seedling? Is the seedling big like a tree or small? Can you find where the roots go? What is the seedling growing in?',
 ask:'"What are the three things a plant needs?" "Which part of the plant drinks?" "What do leaves catch to cook plant food?" "What did our dark-cupboard bean tell us?"',
 childSay:'"The cupboard bean went pale because it was hungry for light!" "Leaves are the kitchen and roots are the straws." Your child may appoint themselves head waterer, check the two jars before breakfast, or explain at a garden centre why the shaded shelf plants look stretchy.',
 real:'Plant needs are visible in every home and street: the drooping pot everyone forgot, the leaning office plant chasing the window, the brown patch where the trampoline shaded the grass. Once your child knows the three needs, wilting becomes a clue, not a mystery.',
 play:'Three-needs sorting game: draw or place three picture cards — water, light, soil — and take turns naming a plant (cactus! water lily! oak tree!) while your child points to what that plant loves most. Then water a real plant together, slowly, at the roots.',
 check:'Ask: "What three things does a plant need? Which part drinks? What did we change in our bean test?" Water-light-soil, roots, the light = landed.',
 tips:'Bean jars beat seeds-in-pots for small scientists because the roots are visible through the glass. Label each jar with a face — happy bean, cupboard bean — and let your child draw the daily verdict; the drawing IS the observation record.'},
'seed':{
 title:'Seed',one:'A packed lunch with a plant inside — soak it, plant it against the glass and catch the first root waking.',
 seoTitle:'Seed Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach Sprouting (Class 31)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what waits inside a seed, why soaking wakes it up, and a clear-jar experiment where roots grow where your child can watch.',
 intro:'This lesson opens a package your child can hold: a seed is a baby plant with its lunch packed, waiting for two signals — warmth and water. Soak one, press it against clear glass, and the first root arrives where they can see it.',
 what:'A seed carries a tiny folded-up plant and a packed lunch of stored food, all inside one shell. When water soaks in and the weather warms, the shell splits, a root pushes down, a shoot pushes up, and the lunch feeds the journey until leaves take over.',
 mean:'The seed is patient on purpose: sprouting into a cold snap would be the end, so it waits for warmth and damp — the go signal. Everything the sprout needs for its first days is already inside, which is why a seed can wake up in a jar with nothing but wet cotton.',
 know:'Soaking a seed overnight swells it and starts the wake-up. Some seeds ride the wind or hitch on animal fur before they land; others wait years underground for their moment. Beans are honest performers — big seeds, fast sprouting, visible roots.',
 why:'Seeds make growth visible from day zero: your child plants it, marks the date, and witnesses the root before any leaf exists. The waiting is the skill — checking daily, drawing changes, learning that living things start small and unfold on their own schedule.',
 dev:'Patience and delayed observation (a real wait with daily checks), sequencing (root first, then shoot), early recording through date marks and drawings, and the wonder fact that a whole plant folds inside something palm-sized.',
 vocab:'seed, sprout, root, shoot, soil, grow',
 teach:'1) Show the card: what is the child planting, and what is the green thing beside them? Name seed and sprout — before and after.\n2) Open the idea: a seed is a lunchbox with a baby plant folded inside, waiting for water and warmth to say go.\n3) Soak a bean overnight and compare: swollen versus dry. That puffing is the seed drinking its wake-up drink.\n4) Press the bean against the glass inside a clear jar, packed round with damp cotton, date-mark the jar, and guess what comes first — root or shoot?\n5) Check every day and draw what appears: root down, shoot up, then leaves. The lunch is eaten, the plant cooks for itself now.',
 say:'"Inside this little seed there is a baby plant, curled up tiny, with its first meals packed around it. Give it a long drink and some warmth, and it wakes: first a root to anchor and sip, then a shoot to find the sun. We do not make it grow — we just give it the go signal and watch."',
 notice:'Ask your child: what is the child planting? Can you find the seed in the picture? What is growing beside the child — is it green? Is the soil dark or pale?',
 ask:'"What is hiding inside a seed?" "What tells a seed it is time to wake up?" "Which comes out first, a root or a shoot?" "Why did our bean get fat overnight?"',
 childSay:'"The seed drank and got fat!" "The root came first — I was right!" Your child may soak everything in sight "to see if it wakes", plant a jar garden on every windowsill, or explain that a seed is "a lunchbox with a plant inside".',
 real:'Seeds are everywhere once you look: apple cores, sunflower packets, the dandelion clock your child blows, grass seeds under the mower. A packet of dried beans from the kitchen shelf is a full experiment kit — and sprouted beans can go straight into a pot of soil.',
 play:'Seed-sort kitchen botany: tip out a spoonful from the cupboard — beans, lentils, sunflower seeds, rice — and sort by size and colour, guessing which are seeds with plants inside. Then plant the biggest ones in a damp-cotton jar and race two kinds to a first root.',
 check:'Ask: "What is inside a seed? What wakes it up? What grows first?" A baby plant and its lunch, water and warmth, the root = landed.',
 tips:'Clear jars pressed against the glass show the root weeks before any pot would — always plant one "for watching". Keep cotton damp, not swimming; mould is the jar garden\u2019s only real enemy. If a seed fails, plant another: failures are data too.'},
'tree':{
 title:'Tree Parts',one:'Roots drink, trunk lifts, leaves cook — walk up a big tree part by part and read the whole machine.',
 seoTitle:'Tree Parts Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach Trees (Class 31)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what roots, trunk and leaves each do, with a bark-rubbing, leaf-collecting tree inspection walk to try this week.',
 intro:'This lesson upgrades the biggest plant your child knows from background scenery to a machine with named parts: roots that drink, a trunk that lifts, leaves that cook — all found on one walk, all touchable, one of them rub-able with crayons.',
 what:'A tree is built in three working parts: roots spread underground, drinking water and holding everything up; the trunk is the lift shaft hauling water to the crown; the leaves up top are the kitchen where sunshine turns into tree food.',
 mean:'Every part has one job and the jobs chain together: drink, lift, cook. A tree is the plant-needs lesson standing up — water from below, light from above — scaled to a size your child can hug, and it lives in one place for decades doing the same three jobs.',
 know:'On a hot day a big tree hauls hundreds of buckets of water up its trunk. Roots peek above soil at the base — easy to find on old trees. Bark is the trunk\u2019s armour, and every kind of tree wears its own pattern: rough oak, peeling birch, smooth beech.',
 why:'Naming parts turns any tree into a readable system your child owns: here is the drinking bit, the lifting bit, the cooking bit. It scales the plant idea up, sets up bark-and-leaf collecting as a science hobby, and makes every street walk a tree tour.',
 dev:'System thinking with parts and jobs, tactile observation (bark rubbings, leaf textures), sorting and comparing collected leaves, and scale — the same plant plan, from bean jar to giant oak.',
 vocab:'tree, roots, trunk, bark, leaves, branches',
 teach:'1) Show the card: who is standing next to what, and is the tree taller than the child? Name the three parts — roots, trunk, leaves.\n2) Assign the jobs: roots drink underground, the trunk lifts the water all the way up, the leaves catch sunshine and cook.\n3) Find a big tree and inspect the base: can you see roots peeking out of the soil? That is the drinking crew, showing up for work.\n4) Rub the trunk: paper and a flat crayon over the bark print its armour pattern. Compare with the next tree — same pattern or different?\n5) Collect three fallen leaves — different shapes welcome — and finish the chain out loud: drink, lift, cook. The whole tree in three verbs.',
 say:'"A tree is a three-part machine. The roots drink water from the soil, the trunk is the lift that carries the water up, up, up, and the leaves at the top catch sunshine and cook the tree\u2019s food. Bark is the trunk\u2019s armour — rub your crayon over it and it writes you its pattern."',
 notice:'Ask your child: what is standing beside the child? Is the tree taller than the child? Can you find the trunk? Where are the leaves — up high or down low? What is the child looking at?',
 ask:'"Which part of the tree drinks?" "What does the trunk carry, and where to?" "What do the leaves do all summer?" "What is the bark for?"',
 childSay:'"The trunk is a water lift!" "I can see the roots — they came out to wave!" Your child may hug every big tree on the walk, collect leaves by the carrier-bagful, or point out drinking roots and lifting trunks wherever trees grow.',
 real:'Trees are the fixed landmarks of childhood: the climbing tree, the conker tree, the one outside the window. Once parts have names, a walk becomes an inspection route — and a fallen big branch shows the rings inside, one ring per year, like a birthday count.',
 play:'Be the tree: your child crouches as roots (fingers wiggling underground), rises as the trunk (arms lifting, up, up), and bursts as leaves (hands fluttering at the top). Call the parts in any order; then swap and let them call your tree parts.',
 check:'Ask: "What do roots do? What does the trunk do? What do leaves do?" Drink, lift, cook = landed.',
 tips:'Fallen leaves only — picking live leaves is stealing the tree\u2019s kitchen, and your child can respect that. Bark rubbings work best on smooth bark with the paper taped down; label each rubbing with the tree\u2019s spot so the collection becomes a map.'},
'cactus':{
 title:'Cactus',one:'A desert plant with a water bottle for a stem — how a cactus saves every drop where rain forgets to fall.',
 seoTitle:'Cactus Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach Desert Plants (Class 31)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how a cactus stores water in its stem, why spines are really leaves, and a sponge-squeeze model of desert survival.',
 intro:'This lesson takes the plant idea somewhere dry: if the rain almost never comes, how does anything stay alive? Your child meets the cactus as a survival engineer — a bottle-stem, needle-leaves, wax jacket — and squeezes a sponge to feel exactly why it works.',
 what:'A cactus is a plant built for the desert: its thick green stem swells up like a bottle and stores water for the dry weeks, its waxy skin seals moisture in, and its spines are actually leaves, shrunk to needles so they waste almost no water.',
 mean:'Every plant needs water — the cactus just solved the problem differently. Where a garden plant drinks daily, the cactus drinks rarely and saves hard: wide stem to store, wax to seal, needles instead of thirsty flat leaves. Different home, different body, same needs.',
 know:'A full cactus can live on its stored water for months. Big desert cacti can weigh as much as a car or more — and most of that weight is water they saved. The spines double as armour: too sharp for thirsty animals to take a bite of the bottle.',
 why:'The cactus stretches the plant-needs lesson into a new climate and plants the adaptation idea: living things fit their homes. It also answers the desert question — how? — with a body plan your child can model with two sponges in thirty seconds.',
 dev:'Cause-and-effect in a new habitat, adaptation thinking (bodies match homes), simple modelling with sponges, and careful-observation habits: where ARE the leaves on this plant, and why?',
 vocab:'cactus, desert, water, stem, spines, store',
 teach:'1) Show the card: what is the child looking at, and what is the cactus growing in — grass or sand? Name the desert home.\n2) Ask the big question: it almost never rains there. How does anything drink? Collect guesses, then open the secret: the stem is a bottle.\n3) Find the leaves — trick question! They became the spines: needles so tiny they hardly lose any water, and too sharp for anyone to nibble.\n4) Run the sponge test: soak one sponge, leave one dry, squeeze both. Full sponge, full cactus — that is what a swollen stem means.\n5) Finish with the wax: cactus skin is sealed like a buttered jacket, holding the water in. Bottle, needles, wax jacket — three tricks, one survivor.',
 say:'"In the desert the rain forgets to come, so the cactus saves every drop it gets. Its fat stem swells up like a water bottle, its skin is sealed so the water cannot sneak out, and its leaves turned into needles — tiny, so they hardly drink at all. When rain finally falls, the bottle fills again."',
 notice:'Ask your child: what is the child looking at? Is the cactus tall or short? Can you find the spines? What is the cactus growing out of — grass, or dry sand?',
 ask:'"What does a cactus keep inside its fat stem?" "Why are the leaves needles instead of flat?" "How long can a cactus live on one big drink?" "Why does nothing try to eat the cactus?"',
 childSay:'"The cactus is a water bottle plant!" "Its leaves turned into needles — that is so clever!" Your child may squeeze sponges at bath time explaining the desert, or point out cactus stems as bottles in every book, garden centre and cartoon.',
 real:'Desert thinking travels: camels store, seals thicken, your winter coat holds warm air — bodies solve their home\u2019s biggest problem. House cacti bring it home literally: your child knows why the windowsill cactus gets one small drink and then is left alone for weeks.',
 play:'Sponge desert: soak two sponges, leave two dry, and line all four on a tray. Squeeze each in turn — which drip, which dust? Then build a cactus from a stuffed sock standing in a sand tray: fat bottle-stem, wax-paper jacket, and cocktail-stick spines only with a grown-up.',
 check:'Ask: "What does a cactus store in its stem? What did its leaves become? Why the waxy skin?" Water, spines, to hold moisture in = landed.',
 safety:'Cactus spines are real needles — house cacti stay up high where small hands cannot reach, and touching one is a grown-up job with tweezers on standby. The craft version follows the same rule: spines point up, fingers stay on the tray edge.',
 tips:'The two-sponge squeeze is the whole lesson in the palm of a hand — do it twice. If a garden centre is nearby, walk the cactus shelf and let your child find the fattest bottle; watering rules can wait, the body plan is today\u2019s win.'},
'mushroom':{
 title:'Mushroom',one:'Not a plant, not an animal — meet the fungus that pops up overnight and lives a secret life underground.',
 seoTitle:'Mushroom Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach Fungi (Class 31)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: why mushrooms are fungi and not plants, the damp-places rule for finding them, and the look-never-taste walk rules.',
 intro:'This lesson breaks the plant-animal pattern: a mushroom is the fruiting cap of a hidden fungus threading through damp soil and old wood. Your child learns where to predict them, what the hidden part does — and the one rule that is never up for negotiation.',
 what:'A mushroom is not a plant — it is one piece of a fungus, a living thing in its own category. Underground and inside old logs, thread-like fungus strands spread through damp places; when the time is right, a mushroom cap pops up to spread spores.',
 mean:'Fungi are neither plant nor animal: they cannot cook their food with sunlight like plants, and they do not eat like animals — they soak up food from damp, decaying places. The mushroom you see is like the apple of a tree whose real body is hidden below.',
 know:'Mushrooms love damp: shady grass, rain-soft soil, old logs — after rain is mushroom o\u2019clock. The cap opens like an umbrella to let spores drift off on the wind. Some wild kinds are poisonous, and that is why the family rule is look-only: shops and grown-ups pick what gets eaten.',
 why:'Mushrooms open a third category in your child\u2019s living-things map — plant, animal, fungus — which is a genuine scientific upgrade. The damp-rule also works as prediction: after rain, your child knows exactly where to look, and being right feels like magic.',
 dev:'Category thinking (a third kind of living thing), habitat prediction (damp spots after rain), respectful-distance discipline with wild things, and drawing or photographing as the way to collect what you must not touch.',
 vocab:'mushroom, fungus, damp, spores, cap, soil',
 teach:'1) Show the card: what is the child crouching beside, and what colour is the cap? Name it — a mushroom, and it is not a plant.\n2) Sort it out: plants cook with sunlight, animals eat, and fungi soak up food from damp, rotting places — a third kind of living thing.\n3) Tell the secret: the real fungus is threads under the ground; the mushroom is its apple, popped up to spread spores like tiny seeds.\n4) Predict together: where would a mushroom like to live — the dry sandpit or the shady corner under the tree? Then test after rain.\n5) Set the rule with its reason: wild mushrooms are never touched or tasted, because some kinds are poisonous, and it is always a grown-up or a shop that decides what gets eaten. We look, we photograph, we draw.',
 say:'"A mushroom is not a plant and not an animal — it is a fungus. The real fungus lives underground as tiny threads, and the mushroom is just its fruit, pushing up when the ground is damp. We look with eyes and camera only — wild mushrooms are never food, because some kinds are poisonous."',
 notice:'Ask your child: what is the child crouching beside? What colour is the mushroom\u2019s cap? Is it growing in the grass? How does the child look — curious, or careful?',
 ask:'"Is a mushroom a plant, an animal, or something else?" "Where do mushrooms like to grow?" "What is hiding under the ground?" "What is our rule about wild mushrooms — and why?"',
 childSay:'"Mushrooms are fungus — a whole third thing!" "The mushroom is the fungus\u2019s apple!" Your child may check the same damp corner after every rain, draw the caps they find instead of picking, or correct a grown-up who calls a mushroom a plant.',
 real:'Mushrooms shop-shelf versus garden-wild is the honest split: button mushrooms in the pack are food, wild caps are look-only. Yeast in bread and the fuzzy mould on forgotten fruit are the same category — fungus is hiding all over a normal week.',
 play:'Spore-print art: with a grown-up, place a shop-bought mushroom cap gills-down on dark paper under a cup overnight — next morning, a spore print! A soft round shadow the fungus made all by itself. Frame it: your child collected something without touching the wild.',
 check:'Ask: "What three kinds of living things have we met? Where do mushrooms grow? Who decides what gets eaten?" Plant-animal-fungus, damp places, grown-ups = landed.',
 safety:'Wild mushrooms are a never-eat, full stop: poisonous kinds can look almost exactly like safe ones, and no clever trick tells them apart — that decision belongs to grown-ups and shops alone. Touching a cap is safe; the habit to build is look, draw, photograph — because at four, hands-off has to hold even when no grown-up is watching.',
 tips:'Wait for a rainy morning, then walk the same short loop — parks, hedge bases, old stumps — and let your child call the damp spots first. A photo collection titled "our mushrooms" beats any bouquet: it grows rain by rain, and nothing wilts.'},
'pond':{
 title:'Pond Habitat',one:'One small pool, many tenants — sit still at the edge and count who shares a pond as their one big home.',
 seoTitle:'Pond Habitat Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach Pond Life (Class 31)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: who lives in a pond and how they share it, with a sit-still-and-count pond watch that teaches habitats at child scale.',
 intro:'This lesson shrinks the whole idea of a habitat down to something a four-year-old can see edge to edge: a pond is one small home where frogs, snails, insects and water plants all fit together — and sitting still reveals more than any stomping.',
 what:'A pond is a small home shared by many living things: frogs hunt insects, snails graze the green slime on stones, water plants give shade and fresh air, and everyone finds food and shelter in the same little pool.',
 mean:'Habitat means the place where an animal finds everything it needs — food, water, shelter — all in one spot. The pond works because the neighbours fit together: plants feed and shade, grazers clean, hunters hunt, and nobody has to leave home to survive.',
 know:'Pond residents come in layers: swimmers below, floaters on the surface, jumpers at the edge, divers at the bottom. Even the water itself is crowded with tiny life. The stillness rule is real biology — loud stomping sends frogs diving; sitting quietly brings the pond back out.',
 why:'A pond is a whole ecosystem at child scale — small enough to see across, busy enough to show sharing. It gathers the set\u2019s animals into one home and teaches the biggest ecology idea gently: animals belong where their needs are met.',
 dev:'Sustained quiet watching (four still minutes is a real achievement), counting and tallying, the habitat idea of needs-met-in-one-place, and respect for shared homes: visitors do not fish, grab or empty the pond.',
 vocab:'pond, habitat, home, frog, snail, plants',
 teach:'1) Show the card: what is the child leaning over, and who is sitting on the lily pad? Name the pond as a home, not just water.\n2) List the tenants: frogs, snails, insects, fish, water plants — everyone shares this one small pool.\n3) Explain the fit: the plants make shade and fresh air, the snails graze the stones clean, the frogs hunt the insects. Neighbours with jobs.\n4) Run the pond-sit: four still minutes, arm\u2019s length from the edge, tallying every living thing you spot — swimmers, floaters, jumpers, gliders.\n5) Compare tallies: stillness finds more than stomping ever will. Ask which neighbours your child would want in their own home — and why the frog never leaves.',
 say:'"A pond is one small home with a lot of tenants. The plants make shade and fresh air, the snails scrub the stones, the insects feed the frogs, and everyone finds dinner without ever leaving home. We sit still like visitors — and the pond forgets we are here and starts its show."',
 notice:'Ask your child: what is the child leaning over? Can you find the frog? What is the frog sitting on? Who else do you think lives in this water?',
 ask:'"Who shares the pond as their home?" "What do the water plants give the pond?" "Why do we sit still at a pond?" "What does a habitat give the animals that live there?"',
 childSay:'"The pond is everyone\u2019s house!" "I counted five things alive — a frog, a snail, and three tiny swimmers!" Your child may play ponds in the paddling pool, assign every toy a pond job, or shush the family at the water\u2019s edge: "quiet — the frogs are coming out."',
 real:'Habitat logic applies everywhere once your child sees it in miniature: a log is a beetle\u2019s pond, a hedge is a wren\u2019s, your garden is one big shared home. The pond version is just the clearest — small, visible edges, tenants on display all summer.',
 play:'Pond in a bowl pretend: blue cloth for water, corks for floating snails, a toy frog on a paper lily pad, cut-out plants for shade. Your child stocks the habitat, then plays the pond-sit: who comes out when the room goes quiet? Add visitors one by one — who eats whom?',
 check:'Ask: "What is a habitat? Who shares a pond? What does stillness find?" A home with everything needed, frogs snails insects plants, more = landed.',
 safety:'Pond edges are the one hard rule: water deeper than a puddle means a grown-up holds a small hand the whole time — slippery banks and deep mud tip little feet faster than anyone expects. The pond-sit happens sitting down, at a distance from the edge, and nothing goes into the water — not hands, not nets, not toes.',
 tips:'Pick a pond with a bench or a flat bank and go at mid-morning, when frogs bask and pond skaters skate. If no pond is near, a wildlife documentary pond scene or a garden water-butt with wrigglers works — the stillness habit is the portable part.'},
'cloud':{
 title:'Cloud',one:'A floating crowd of tiny drops — breathe on a window, make your own cloud and learn when grey means rain.',
 seoTitle:'Cloud Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach Clouds (Class 31)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what clouds are made of, why they float and why grey ones rain, plus a breath-on-the-window mini-cloud to make.',
 intro:'This lesson lifts the weather into reach: clouds are crowds of tiny water drops floating high, and your child can make one on a cold window with a single breath — then read the sky, white for shape-finding, grey for rain incoming.',
 what:'A cloud is made of tiny drops of water floating in the air — so small that the air holds them up. When many drops crowd together, the cloud turns grey and heavy, and the drops join until they are big enough to fall as rain.',
 mean:'Clouds are the sky\u2019s visible water: the sun lifts moisture up as vapour, invisible, and crowds of drops are what it looks like when that vapour gathers high up. White means widely spread and light; grey means packed and ready to pour.',
 know:'A small-looking cloud can weigh as much as a herd of elephants — the drops are just spread across a huge space. Fog is a cloud that has come down to the ground, and you can walk through it. Cloud shapes shift constantly: the crowd is always moving.',
 why:'Clouds answer "where does rain come from?" before it rains, and the breath-on-the-window trick makes the material touchable: your own breath carries water, and it turns to visible drops on cold glass — a cloud in miniature, made by your child.',
 dev:'Sky-watching as a daily habit, shape-finding imagination on moving clouds, cause-and-effect (crowded drops, grey, rain), and the breath experiment\u2019s lesson that invisible water is real and everywhere — including inside us.',
 vocab:'cloud, drops, float, grey, sky, fog',
 teach:'1) Show the card: what is the child pointing at? Is the cloud big or small? Name it — a floating crowd of tiny water drops.\n2) Ask the stumper: clouds are heavy with water — why do they not fall? Because the drops are tiny and the air holds them up high.\n3) Make a mini-cloud: breathe on a cold window or mirror and watch the mist appear — your breath carried water, and the cold glass gathered it into drops.\n4) Read the sky together: white puffy clouds for shape-finding — a bunny, a boat — and grey-packed clouds for rain coming. Which is overhead today?\n5) Drop the fog fact: fog is a cloud that came down to visit. You can walk through a cloud — you probably already have.',
 say:'"A cloud is a crowd of tiny water drops floating high in the sky. Each drop is so small the air holds it up, but when the drops crowd close together the cloud turns grey and heavy — and then the drops tumble down as rain. Breathe on the window: you just made a baby cloud."',
 notice:'Ask your child: what is the child pointing at? Is the cloud big in the sky? What shape does the cloud look like to you? Can you find more clouds — or is the sky nearly clear?',
 ask:'"What are clouds made of?" "Why do clouds float instead of falling?" "What does it mean when a cloud turns grey?" "What did your breath make on the window?"',
 childSay:'"Clouds are made of tiny drops — elephants\u2019 worth!" "I made a cloud on the window with my breath!" Your child may read the sky out loud every morning — "grey today, rain incoming" — or blow on cold windows on purpose to grow mist.',
 real:'Clouds are the daily forecast your child can read: the white-building day for kite flying, the flat grey blanket for wellies, the fog walk to nursery where the world goes soft. Shape games on a picnic blanket, grey-watching before a walk — the sky is the toy.',
 play:'Cotton-wool sky: glue cotton balls onto blue paper — stretched thin for wispy clouds, bunched high for towering ones, pressed flat and grey-tinted for rain clouds. Then lie back on the rug and find shapes in each other\u2019s cotton clouds before checking the real sky.',
 check:'Ask: "What are clouds made of? Why do they float? What does grey mean?" Tiny water drops, the air holds them, rain is coming = landed.',
 tips:'Morning is best for cloud watching — shape-hunting works before the day fills up. The window-breath trick needs genuinely cold glass; on warm days, a fridge-chilled mirror does it. Grey-sky days get a bonus: fog is the walk-through cloud your child can finally touch.'},
'rain':{
 title:'Rain',one:'The sky giving water back — follow a drop from vapour up to cloud to rain, and report the weather from the window.',
 seoTitle:'Rain Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach the Water Cycle (Class 31)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: where rain comes from, how the same water travels round and round, and a window weather report plus puddle map.',
 intro:'This lesson turns a rainy day into the point: rain is the sky returning the water the sun lifted up, and your child can watch the whole trip from the window — then map the puddles it leaves behind and learn that this water has been round this loop forever.',
 what:'Rain happens because sunshine lifts water up — out of seas, puddles and plants — as vapour too small to see. High above, the vapour gathers into clouds; when the drops inside grow heavy, they fall as rain, watering everything below before the trip starts over.',
 mean:'Rain is not the sky leaking — it is one step of a loop: up as vapour, along in clouds, down as drops, away in rivers and puddles, and back up again. The water falling today is the same water that has been going round this planet since long before us.',
 know:'Vapour is water you cannot see — it is floating past right now. A rainy-day drop once splashed on dinosaurs: the loop is that old. Rain lands as drips, drops or downpours, and every puddle is the sky\u2019s delivery, waiting for the sun to lift it back.',
 why:'The water cycle answers the question every child asks — where does rain come from? — honestly and simply, and it makes rainy days purposeful: the garden is being watered, the puddles are a map, and the window is the weather station.',
 dev:'Understanding a circular story (a loop with no beginning or end), weather vocabulary and reporting, observation from a fixed post (the window), and the deep-time wow: same water, dinosaurs, today.',
 vocab:'rain, cloud, drops, vapour, puddle, heavy',
 teach:'1) Show the card: what is the child wearing, and where is the rain coming from? Find the cloud — the rain\u2019s delivery van.\n2) Tell the loop: sunshine turns water into invisible vapour that floats up, clouds gather it, and when the drops inside get heavy, down it comes. Up, along, down, around.\n3) Report from the window: how heavy is the rain — drips, drops, or pouring? Say it like a weather reporter on TV.\n4) Map the puddles after: which is biggest, which dries first, and where did the water go when the sun came back? Up — again.\n5) Land the deep fact: this exact water has been round the loop for millions of years — the rain outside once splashed on dinosaurs.',
 say:'"Sunshine lifts water up out of the sea and the puddles — as vapour, so small you cannot see it. Up high it gathers into clouds, and when the drops get heavy the sky lets them go: rain! Puddles form, the sun lifts them up again, and the whole trip starts over. That water has been going round for ages."',
 notice:'Ask your child: what is the child wearing — a raincoat, or a sun hat? Where is the rain falling from? Can you find the cloud? Is the child watching the rain or running away from it?',
 ask:'"Where does rain come from?" "What is vapour?" "What happens to a puddle when the sun comes out?" "How heavy is the rain today — drips, drops or pouring?"',
 childSay:'"The rain is the water coming back down!" "My puddle got lifted up — I saw it dry!" Your child may deliver window weather reports every wet morning, jump puddles while announcing "the clouds got heavy", or comfort a grey day: "the garden is getting its drink."',
 real:'The loop is visible all week: kettle steam, damp pavement drying after rain, fog on the bathroom mirror — water, changing place and form. Once your child owns the loop, rain stops being a cancelled plan and becomes the most interesting thing the sky does.',
 play:'Water-cycle in a bag: with a grown-up, tape a zip bag with a splash of water high on a sunny window. Mark the water line. Over days, drops climb the bag wall — evaporated up, gathered at the top, and raining back down when heavy enough. The whole loop, hanging in the sun.',
 check:'Ask: "Where does rain come from? What lifts the water up? What happens to puddles in the sun?" Clouds, the sun, they dry back up = landed.',
 tips:'Keep the window post safe and warm — rain watching is an inside sport, with wellies saved for the puddle map afterwards. If thunder joins the rain, come inside and leave the counting to the grown-ups — the loop story keeps for the next dry day.'},
'rain-helps-plants':{
 title:'Rain Helps Plants',one:'The garden\u2019s free watering can — why rainy days are planting days and roots are the straws doing the drinking.',
 seoTitle:'Rain Helps Plants Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach Thirsty Gardens (Class 31)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how rainwater reaches plant roots, why rainy days are garden days, and an after-rain leaf-and-soil tour to take.',
 intro:'This lesson flips the rainy-day grump: while everyone runs inside, the whole garden sits down to drink. Your child learns that rain is a watering can for every plant outside — and takes an after-rain tour to see exactly who has been drinking.',
 what:'Plants are always drinking — their roots sip water from the soil non-stop — and rain refills the soil faster than any hose. Drops land on leaves, trickle down stems, and sink into the dark earth where the roots are waiting to slurp it all up.',
 mean:'Rain is weather on the plant\u2019s side: a whole night of rain does more for the garden than an afternoon of watering, because it soaks deep where roots actually reach. The garden is not getting wet — it is getting fed, and the soil is the cup it drinks from.',
 know:'Roots do the drinking, not the leaves — raindrops on a leaf are just passing through on their way down. After rain the soil turns dark and damp, leaves wear beads of water, and worms come up for the wet. A dry week makes plants droop; rain perks them back up.',
 why:'This card connects weather to growth: rain is not a nuisance, it is lunch for every plant outside. It gives your child a reason to be glad about wet days, links the plant-needs lesson to the sky, and makes the watering chore feel like helping the rain.',
 dev:'Linking weather to plant growth (cause and effect across a whole garden), after-rain observation of soil, leaves and worms, empathy with living things (the garden was thirsty), and patience — the tour works best a day after the rain.',
 vocab:'rain, plants, roots, drink, soil, garden',
 teach:'1) Show the card: what is the seedling getting, and what is the child wearing? Name it — the garden\u2019s watering day.\n2) Ask the flip question: what happens to the plants outside when it rains all day? The garden is not hiding from rain — it is drinking it.\n3) Explain the straws: roots sip constantly from the soil, and rain refills the soil cup deep down where straws reach. Drops on leaves just trickle home.\n4) Take the after-rain tour: find leaves wearing raindrop beads, press a finger into dark damp soil, and count the worms that came up for the wet.\n5) Connect to home: your watering can does for pots what the sky does for gardens — and after a rainy week, the pots get a day off.',
 say:'"When it rains, the whole garden sits down to eat — or really, to drink. The drops run off the leaves, down the stems, into the soil, and the roots slurp it up like straws. A whole night of rain is the best meal the garden ever gets. That is why rainy days are actually garden days."',
 notice:'Ask your child: what is falling on the seedling? What is the child wearing in the picture? Is the seedling big or small? What do you think the seedling will grow into after its drink?',
 ask:'"Which part of a plant drinks the rain?" "Where do the raindrops on a leaf go next?" "What does the soil look like after rain?" "Why are rainy days good for the garden?"',
 childSay:'"Rainy days are garden drinking days!" "The worms came up because they like the wet!" Your child may refuse to grumble on wet days now, check the soil with one finger after rain, or explain to a worried plant-waterer that the sky has the shift covered.',
 real:'Rain\u2019s work shows all around: drooping tomatoes perk up overnight, the lawn greens by the weekend, rain-fed gardens outgrow watered pots. After the next dry spell your child can run the reverse check — which plants missed the sky\u2019s watering can?',
 play:'Watering-can relay: paper plants around the room, one cup of "rain" per runner — sprinkle each plant gently at the base, not the leaves, because roots do the drinking. Then a slow-motion after-rain tour of your own: leaves, soil, and one lucky worm hunt outside.',
 check:'Ask: "Who drinks the rain — leaves or roots? Where does the water collect? What is a rainy day for the garden?" Roots, the soil, a big drink = landed.',
 tips:'Do the finger-test of damp soil together after every rain — one gentle poke, two centimetres down — and your child will soon read the garden\u2019s thirst by touch. Welly weather is tour weather: the wetter the walk, the more the garden shows.'},
'rainbow':{
 title:'Rainbow',one:'Sunlight secretly wearing all the colors — make your own rainbow with a spray bottle and count all seven.',
 seoTitle:'Rainbow Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach Rainbow Colors (Class 31)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how sunlight and raindrops paint the arc, the stand-with-your-back-to-the-sun rule, and a spray-bottle rainbow to make.',
 intro:'This lesson cracks the sky\u2019s best trick: white sunlight is secretly a whole family of colors, and raindrops are the mirrors that split it apart. Your child learns the two-ingredient recipe — sun behind, drops in front — then makes a rainbow on demand with a spray.',
 what:'A rainbow appears when sunlight shines through falling raindrops: each drop splits the white light into its colors, and millions of drops together paint an arc across the sky. Sun AND rain, each in its place, or no rainbow at all.',
 mean:'White light is a mix hiding in plain sight — a raindrop is a tiny prism that unmixes it. The recipe needs both ingredients at once: sun on one side, drops on the other. That is why rainbows visit while rain is leaving, not while it is pouring on your head.',
 know:'Look carefully and a rainbow carries seven colors: red, orange, yellow, green, blue, indigo, violet. The rule for finding one: stand with the sun behind you and the drops in front. The same trick works with a hose mist or a spray bottle on any sunny day.',
 why:'Rainbows are light science your child can point at and even manufacture — the spray-bottle recipe turns waiting for weather into making it. The two-conditions rule (sun behind, drops ahead) is also early cause-and-effect: no ingredients, no rainbow.',
 dev:'Observation of a fleeting phenomenon, counting and naming colors in order, cause-and-effect with two required ingredients, and the maker-mindset: recreating nature\u2019s trick at garden-hose scale.',
 vocab:'rainbow, light, colors, raindrops, sun, arc',
 teach:'1) Show the card: what is the child looking up at, and can you name the colors you see? Count them together, top to bottom.\n2) Tell the secret: sunlight looks white, but it is secretly carrying every color — a raindrop splits it apart like a key opening a box of paints.\n3) Learn the recipe: sun behind you, drops in front of you — both at once, or nothing appears. That is why rainbows come out as rain moves away.\n4) Make your own: on a sunny day, stand with your back to the sun and spray a fine mist into the air — a small rainbow hangs in the water.\n5) Play color spotter: find all seven in the arc, then hunt them off the sky — red postbox, yellow banana, violet plum. The rainbow\u2019s family is everywhere.',
 say:'"Sunlight looks white, but inside it hides every color you can name. When sun shines through raindrops, each little drop splits the light like a prism — and a whole arc of drops paints the rainbow across the sky. Want the recipe? Sun behind you, rain in front. We can even make one with the spray bottle!"',
 notice:'Ask your child: what is the child looking up at? How many colors can you find in the rainbow? Can you name one of them? Does the child look happy to see it?',
 ask:'"What two things do you need to make a rainbow?" "Where does the sun need to be standing?" "What does a raindrop do to white light?" "How many colors live in a rainbow?"',
 childSay:'"Sunlight has all the colors hiding inside!" "Sun behind, drops in front — I can make one!" Your child may carry the spray bottle on every sunny walk, demand rainbow checks during every shower, or name the seven colors in order like a spell.',
 real:'Rainbow conditions show up weekly: sun shower, spray at the fountains, the hose on the lawn. Once your child knows the recipe, they stop waiting and start engineering — back to the sun, mist in the air, and there it hangs. Bubbles and CDs carry the same color-splitting trick indoors.',
 play:'Prism stations: a glass of water on a sunny windowsill throws a small rainbow on the floor; a CD under a lamp scatters colors; bubbles wear moving ones. Send your child on a rainbow hunt around the house — where else does light get split apart?',
 check:'Ask: "What two things make a rainbow? Where does the sun stand? What does a drop do to light?" Sun and raindrops, behind you, splits it into colors = landed.',
 tips:'The spray-bottle rainbow takes a try or two — fine mist, low sun, and your back squarely to it. Morning and evening sun make the biggest arcs. If the real sky delivers one, drop everything and run out: a first wild rainbow is a core memory.'},
'wind':{
 title:'Wind',one:'Air you cannot see, pushing everything you can — become a wind detective and catch five moving things.',
 seoTitle:'Wind Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach Moving Air (Class 31)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how we know wind is there when air is invisible, with a find-five-moving-things detective game and a streamer test.',
 intro:'This lesson cracks the hardest idea a preschooler meets — invisible things are real — with the friendliest possible evidence: the wind. Your child cannot see air, but they can see everything it pushes, feel it on their face, and hold a streamer up to catch it working.',
 what:'Wind is air on the move. Air itself stays invisible, but everything it pushes gives it away: kites lifting, leaves tumbling, flags snapping, hair flying, washing swinging. A gentle breeze is a small push; a gust is a big one.',
 mean:'You cannot see the wind, but you can see what it does — that is evidence, the scientist\u2019s way of knowing something without eyes on it. Still air is real too: it is what your child is breathing right now, and it only shows itself when it moves.',
 know:'Moving air does real work: it pushes boats with sails, spins windmills, dries the washing and lifts kites high. A paper streamer hangs limp in still air and streams flat when the wind blows — a wind meter made from one strip of paper.',
 why:'Wind teaches proof-by-effect: how do you know something invisible is there? Watch what it moves. That habit — evidence over eyesight — transfers to sound, gravity and germs later, and it makes every windy walk a science field.',
 dev:'Evidence-based reasoning (seeing effects, not the thing), weather awareness, cause-and-effect at speed (gusts come and go), and physical play — leaning into wind, catching leaves, feeling pushes on cheeks and chest.',
 vocab:'wind, air, push, breeze, kite, leaves',
 teach:'1) Look at the card: what is the child holding, and what is lifting the kite? Trick question — can you SEE the wind itself?\n2) Set the detective case: the wind is invisible, so how do we know it is there? Because we can see everything it pushes.\n3) Play the moving hunt: outdoors, tally five things the wind is pushing — leaves, flags, hair, washing, clouds. Count them out loud.\n4) Make the meter: a paper streamer on a stick — hanging means still air, streaming means the wind is working. Test it high and low.\n5) Feel the proof: stand into the breeze, cheeks out. The push you feel is air, and air is real even when it stands still — you are breathing some right now.',
 say:'"Nobody has ever seen the wind — but we can see everything it pushes! Kites lift, leaves tumble, flags flap, your hair goes everywhere. When you feel a push on your face, that is air moving past. Even still air is real: you are breathing it in and out right now."',
 notice:'Ask your child: what is in the child\u2019s hands? Is the kite going up or coming down? What do you think is lifting the kite? Can you see the wind itself anywhere in the picture?',
 ask:'"What is the wind made of?" "How do we know the wind is there if we cannot see it?" "What did your streamer do in still air?" "What can strong wind push — a leaf or a boat?"',
 childSay:'"I cannot see the wind but I can see it PUSHING!" "Still air hangs my streamer down." Your child may appoint themselves official wind reporter on every walk, hold paper streamers out on the balcony, or explain that wind is "air on the run".',
 real:'Wind signs are everywhere once you are the detective: drying washing, rustling hedges, dust drifting down the street, clouds racing overhead. Windmills and sailboats are the big-league versions — moving air doing heavy work, exactly like your child\u2019s kite.',
 play:'Wind duel: hold a feather race — who can blow their feather across the table using only breath-wind? Then fan-test: paper plate fans, curtain waves, a huffing-puffing relay. Finish outside on the next gusty day with a homemade streamer on a stick.',
 check:'Ask: "What is wind? How do we know it is there? What did the streamer show?" Moving air, the pushes we can see, still or streaming = landed.',
 tips:'The streamer meter is the keep-forever tool — tape one to the garden fence or balcony rail and your child reads the wind over breakfast. Gusty days are kite days; still days are feather-breath days. Both are the same lesson at two speeds.'},
'ice':{
 title:'Ice Cubes',one:'Water wearing its winter shape — freeze it, melt it, and race one cube on a warm palm against one on a cool plate.',
 seoTitle:'Ice Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach Freezing and Melting (Class 31)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: ice as water changed by cold, melting as warmth letting it change back, and a warm-palm-versus-cool-plate melt race.',
 intro:'This lesson makes states of matter touchable — literally: one substance, two shapes, back and forth on demand. Your child freezes water, melts it again, and runs the melt race that shows cold making solid and warmth setting it free.',
 what:'Ice is water that got so cold it froze into a solid — the same water, wearing a hard shape. Add warmth and the ice gives up, melts, and turns back into liquid water. Cold freezes, warmth melts; back and forth as many times as you like.',
 mean:'One substance, two costumes: water is a liquid that pours, ice is the same water standing still. Nothing is lost in the change — a vanished ice cube is a puddle in disguise, which is why melting is not disappearing, just changing back.',
 know:'Water is the everyday thing that shows up in all three forms: solid ice, liquid water, and invisible vapour in the air. Ice floats in your drink because frozen water puffs up slightly bigger than it was. Freezers make ice by stealing heat; warm hands give it back.',
 why:'Ice is the friendliest states-of-matter lesson there is: your kitchen can run it daily, the change is visible, fast and reversible. It builds cold-warm causality — temperature as a cause with visible effects — and the conservation idea that stuff does not vanish.',
 dev:'Cause-and-effect with temperature, reversible-change thinking (freeze and melt, freeze again), prediction and fair testing in the melt race, and vocabulary — solid, melt, freeze — that will carry through every later science lesson.',
 vocab:'ice, water, freeze, melt, cold, warm',
 teach:'1) Show the card: what is the child holding? Does it look cold or warm? Name it — water\u2019s hard, frozen costume.\n2) Run the freeze: fill a tray with water, pop it in the freezer, and check an hour later. Same water — where did the wobble go?\n3) Explain the trade: cold freezes water solid, warmth melts it back. Nothing disappears — a "vanished" cube is a puddle in disguise.\n4) Race the melt: one cube on a warm palm, one on a cool plate — which gives up first? Why? Warmth is the melt-maker, so the palm wins.\n5) Find the puddle under the loser and ask where the ice went: it changed back. Freeze it again tonight — the show repeats forever.',
 say:'"Ice is just water that got very, very cold — the same water, frozen into a hard little shape. Hold it in your warm hand and the warmth melts it back into water: it did not disappear, it changed its costume! Cold freezes, warmth melts, and you can run the trick all day."',
 notice:'Ask your child: what is the child holding? Does the ice cube look big or small? What do you think the ice feels like — bumpy, smooth, slippery? Where do you think the child got it?',
 ask:'"What is ice made of?" "What melts ice back into water?" "Where did the ice cube go when it vanished?" "What does the freezer do to water?"',
 childSay:'"The ice didn\u2019t vanish — it melted back into water!" "My hand is a melt-machine!" Your child may run melt races on every warm surface, rescue cubes from the freezer to "free" them, or explain that winter puddles are "just water wearing its cold costume".',
 real:'Ice works everywhere: drinks clinking in summer, frost on the grass in winter, the freezer\u2019s cold boxes on picnics. Melting is daily too — ice lollies drip, frost fades off the car windscreen, and snowman puddles tell the same story every January.',
 play:'Ice treasure rescue: freeze small toys into a tub of water, then hand your child a cup of warm water and a spoon to excavate them. Warmth melts, tools chip, treasures pop free. Afterwards, leave one chunk on the plate and watch it confess: it was water all along.',
 check:'Ask: "What is ice? What does warmth do to it? Where does a melted cube go?" Frozen water, melts it, back to being water = landed.',
 tips:'Freeze treasure tubs the night before — the rescue buys twenty focused minutes, and the warm-water spoon teaches control. Keep the melt race fair: same-size cubes, started at the same moment. Your palm wins, but the cool plate teaches the patience half of warmth.'},
'rock':{
 title:'Rock',one:'Pieces of Earth you can pocket — collect five, sort them your way and meet the minerals sparkling inside.',
 seoTitle:'Rock Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach Rock Sorting (Class 31)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what rocks are, how pebbles get smooth, and a collect-and-sort rock hunt that turns any path into a museum.',
 intro:'This lesson starts the cheapest science hobby on Earth: rock collecting. Your child gathers five stones, breaks the all-rocks-are-grey assumption, and sorts a pocketful by feel, weight and shine — classification practice with a museum that fits in a shoebox.',
 what:'Rocks are hard pieces of Earth itself — the ground is made of them. Some rocks cooled from hot melted rock, some were pressed in layers over ages, and pebbles are rocks that water and weather rolled smooth. Inside many rocks, sparkling specks called minerals hide.',
 mean:'Rocks are not all alike — they differ in color, texture, weight and shine, and sorting those differences is real science. Every pebble is a story: a sharp rock sanded round by years of waves, rain and rolling until it fits perfectly in a small palm.',
 know:'Rocks come from different recipes: cooled lava, pressed mud layers, and minerals that sparkle when the light catches them. A smooth beach pebble took ages to tumble round. Geologists — rock scientists — read those clues to tell Earth\u2019s story.',
 why:'Rock sorting is classification practice with instant feedback: bumpy or smooth, heavy or light, grey or sparkly — and no two collections match. It also grounds your child literally: the ground under everything has names, histories and surprises.',
 dev:'Classification by properties (color, texture, weight, shine), close looking with a magnifier, collecting and caring for a set, and the long-time idea — smoothness that takes years, layers that take longer.',
 vocab:'rock, pebble, smooth, hard, mineral, sort',
 teach:'1) Show the card: what is the child examining, and what are they looking through? Is the rock smooth or bumpy? Name the tool — a magnifying glass.\n2) Break the assumption: are all rocks grey? Hunt for pink, white, speckled, striped — rocks are Earth\u2019s mixed recipe box.\n3) Collect five: parks, paths, beaches — five different stones, each one chosen for a reason. Why THIS one?\n4) Sort your own way: by color, by feel, by weight, by shine — then re-sort by a new rule. Scientists sort and re-sort all the time.\n5) Look close: specks that sparkle are minerals, the ingredients inside. And the smoothest pebble? Water rolled it round for years before your child found it.',
 say:'"Rocks are pieces of the Earth itself — the whole ground is made of them. Some came from hot melted rock gone hard, some were pressed in layers, and every smooth pebble was rolled round by water over years and years. Look inside: those sparkling specks are minerals, the ingredients rocks are cooked from."',
 notice:'Ask your child: what is the child examining? What are they looking through? Is the rock smooth or bumpy? What color is the rock — grey, or something else?',
 ask:'"What is a rock a piece of?" "How did the smooth pebble get so smooth?" "What are the sparkling specks inside rocks called?" "How many ways can you sort your five rocks?"',
 childSay:'"Minerals are the sparkles inside!" "My pebble got smooth from the waves rolling it!" Your child may start a shoebox museum with labelled favourites, sort every gravel patch on the walk, or hand you the heaviest rock and ask you to guess its weight.',
 real:'Rocks are the floor of childhood: driveways, riverbanks, the beach, the gravel by the school gate. The collection grows every trip, and the sorting never ends — by weight, by shine, by which one looks most like a potato. Museums upgrade it later; the habit starts now.',
 play:'Pebble-washing day: rinse and brush the collection in a basin, lay it out on a towel, and sort into trays — sparkles, stripes, smoothest, heaviest. Then a tumbler demo: a jar, a splash of sand and one sugar cube, shaken gently, shows edges wearing round like the sea does it.',
 check:'Ask: "What are rocks pieces of? What made the pebble smooth? What are the sparkles called?" Earth, water rolling it, minerals = landed.',
 tips:'Set a pick-up limit before the walk — five is plenty — and a display box after. A cheap magnifying glass doubles the pleasure: sparkles, stripes and sand-grains are all hiding in plain pebbles. Say yes to the pocketful; the collecting instinct is the science.'},
'sun':{
 title:'The Sun',one:'Our nearest star, giving light and warmth — feel the heat on a sunny wall and learn the one rule that keeps eyes safe.',
 seoTitle:'Sun Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach the Sun (Class 31)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: the sun as our nearest star, how its light and warmth reach us, and the never-look-straight-at-it rule with its reason.',
 intro:'This lesson names the engine behind nearly everything your child has learned: plants, weather, shadows, day and night — all run on the sun. One star, 150 million kilometres away, delivers light and warmth straight to the palm of a hand.',
 what:'The sun is a star — a giant ball of burning-bright gas — and the closest one to Earth. It pours out light and warmth in every direction; a small share lands on us, and that share makes our daytime, grows our food and warms our skin.',
 mean:'Every other star is a much farther sun: pinpricks of light at night are the same kind of giant ball, just too far to feel. The sun is the engine behind the garden — plants cook with its light, puddles dry from its warmth, and shadows point away from it.',
 know:'The sun\u2019s warmth travels about 150 million kilometres to reach your hand — and you can feel it. Light and warmth are gifts, but staring is not: looking straight at the sun damages eyes, and sunglasses do not make staring safe. Shade, hats and looking away are the rule.',
 why:'The sun ties the whole science set together — plants, weather, shadows, the moon\u2019s shine, day and night all borrow its power. Naming it as a star upgrades the night sky: your child now knows that stars are suns, and one of them is ours.',
 dev:'Connecting earlier lessons to one power source (plants, weather, shadows), scale thinking (a star we can feel versus stars we only see), sensory evidence for warmth, and safety rules with reasons that stick.',
 vocab:'sun, star, light, warmth, shadow, shade',
 teach:'1) Show the card: what is the child doing with their hands, and why? Shading the eyes — exactly what smart sun-watchers do.\n2) Name it: the sun is a star, the closest one — every other star in the night sky is a far-off sun of its own.\n3) Feel the delivery: press one palm flat on a sun-baked wall, one on a shaded wall. Feel that difference? The warmth crossed 150 million kilometres to get here.\n4) Trace the power: plants cook with sun light, puddles dry in sun warmth, shadows point away from the sun. One star, half the set\u2019s lessons.\n5) Set the rule with its reason: the sun is too bright to look at — staring can damage young eyes for good, and sunglasses do not make staring safe. We feel the sun; we never stare.',
 say:'"The sun is our star — the closest one, a huge ball of burning light and warmth. Its light makes our day, its warmth grows the plants, and you can feel it right on your hand. But it is too bright for eyes: staring is off the table, sunglasses or not. We enjoy the sun by feeling it and looking away."',
 notice:'Ask your child: what is the child doing with their hands? Is the sun smiling in the picture? Is the child smiling too? Why do you think the child is shading their eyes instead of looking right at it?',
 ask:'"What is the sun — a planet or a star?" "What does the sun give us?" "How far did the warmth on your hand travel?" "Why do we never stare at the sun?"',
 childSay:'"The sun is a star — OUR star!" "Every star is a sun, just far, far away!" Your child may feel walls, pavements and railings for sun warmth and report the heat, shade their eyes like a salute on every sunny walk, or correct anyone who calls the sun a planet.',
 real:'Sun power is daily life: washing drying, tomatoes ripening, the hot slide at noon, the cold side of the house in shade. Once your child names the sun as the engine, the garden becomes a powered system — and the night sky becomes a field of far-off suns to wonder at.',
 play:'Warmth treasure hunt: find five warm things the sun made — a sunny wall, a metal railing, the car bonnet, a paving stone, the sandbox — and five cool things in shade. Rank them hottest to coolest. The sun\u2019s fingerprints, collected by hand.',
 check:'Ask: "What is the sun? What does it give us? Can we look straight at it?" Our nearest star, light and warmth, never — and now we know why = landed.',
 safety:'The sun is a never-stare rule, full stop: looking straight at it burns the light-catching back of the eye, and the damage is permanent — sunglasses are for comfort, not permission. Shade and hats do the protecting on bright days, and the warm-wall test is the safe way to study the sun: feel it, never face it.',
 tips:'Run the two-wall warmth compare on every sunny day — the palm test never gets old. Midday summer sun is hat, shade and water-bottle weather; the science lesson pairs perfectly with the practical one. Save star-gazing for after dark: the night sky is the sun\u2019s family photo.'},
'moon':{
 title:'Moon',one:'A rock that shines with borrowed light — start a moon diary tonight and catch the shape changing all month.',
 seoTitle:'Moon Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach Moon Watching (Class 31)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: why the moon shines with borrowed sunlight, how its shape changes across a month, and a draw-it-every-night moon diary.',
 intro:'This lesson hands your child the sky object they can actually watch change: the moon. No telescope needed — a window, a diary and a month of checking reveals borrowed light, changing shapes, and the day-shift moon that sometimes shows up before bed.',
 what:'The moon is a rocky ball circling Earth, and it glows only with borrowed light — sunlight lands on it and bounces back, like a torch on a mirror. As the month goes round, we see the lit part from different angles: crescent, half, full.',
 mean:'Moonlight is second-hand sunlight: the moon is a reflector, not a lamp. The changing shape is not the moon being nibbled — it is the same rocky ball, lit from one side, while we watch from a moving Earth. The shapes are geometry your child can see for free.',
 know:'The moon has no wind and no rain, so astronauts\u2019 footprints from decades ago are still pressed in its dust, fresh as the day they landed. Some nights the moon hides; some mornings it takes the early shift, hanging pale in the daytime sky.',
 why:'The moon is the nightly observation habit your child can own — no equipment, one drawing a day. Learning that its shine is borrowed links the moon back to the sun, and the changing shapes teach that watching over time is how you see slow things happen.',
 dev:'Long-horizon observation (a month-long diary), recording with drawings, the reflection idea (shining by borrowed light), and comfort with the moon\u2019s pattern — the same shapes return, which is what makes them predictable.',
 vocab:'moon, night, light, crescent, full, sky',
 teach:'1) Show the card: what is the child looking through, and what are they looking at? Name it — Earth\u2019s rocky companion.\n2) Ask the trick question: does the moon make its own light? No — the sun shines on it and the moon bounces the light back, like a mirror.\n3) Start the moon diary: draw its shape tonight — crescent, half, or full? Date every drawing. Some nights it hides; those count too.\n4) Check the early shift: look for the moon before bed AND in the morning — sometimes it hangs pale in the daytime sky.\n5) Explain the shapes: nothing is eating the moon — it is one ball, lit from one side, and we see the lit part from different angles as the month goes round.',
 say:'"The moon does not glow by itself — it is a big rocky ball, and the sun lights it up like a torch shining on a mirror. That is why its shape seems to change: we see the sunny side from different angles all month. And get this — the footprints astronauts left there are still there, because the moon has no wind to blow them away."',
 notice:'Ask your child: what is the child looking through? What can they see in the sky? Is the picture set at night or in the day? What shape is the moon in the picture?',
 ask:'"Does the moon make its own light?" "Where does the moon\u2019s light come from?" "Why does the moon seem to change shape?" "Why are the astronauts\u2019 footprints still there?"',
 childSay:'"The moon is a mirror for the sun!" "No wind, no rain — the footprints stay forever!" Your child may guard their moon diary like a treasure, report the moon\u2019s shape from the car window, or shout "day-shift moon!" when it shows up over breakfast.',
 real:'The moon is nightly, free and impossible to miss once your child is looking: dog-walk crescents, bath-time full moons, the pale daytime moon over the school run. The diary turns "always there" into "always changing on a schedule" — a pattern your child can crack in one month.',
 play:'Ball-and-torch moon: in a dim room, shine a torch (the sun) on a ball (the moon) and let your child walk around it, watching the lit shape change from crescent to full as the angle changes. Then draw tonight\u2019s real moon and compare it to the model.',
 check:'Ask: "Does the moon make its own light? Why does its shape change? What makes the moon shine?" No — borrowed, angles, sunlight bouncing back = landed.',
 tips:'Check the moon at the same time each evening for a week before switching times — the shape changes are clearest on a fixed schedule. A torch-and-ball session answers the shape question better than words. Missing-moon nights go in the diary too: hiding is part of the pattern.'},
'day-and-night':{
 title:'Day and Night',one:'The planet is turning, not the sun — spin a ball in torchlight and see exactly where night comes from.',
 seoTitle:'Day and Night Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach Earth\u2019s Spin (Class 31)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: why Earth\u2019s spin makes day and night, where the sun really goes at night, and a flashlight-on-a-ball model to try.',
 intro:'This lesson answers the classic bedtime question — where does the sun GO at night? — with the biggest idea in the set: the sun goes nowhere. Earth spins, and your side turning away from the light is what night is. A torch and a ball make it visible.',
 what:'Day and night come from Earth spinning like a top: one full turn takes 24 hours, and that turn is what a day means. The side of Earth facing the sun has day; the side turned away has night. We are riding the spin, so the light sweeps past us every 24 hours.',
 mean:'The sun does not rise or set — it shines steadily while the ground under your feet turns. Sunrise is your spot on Earth rotating into the light; sunset is it rotating out. And bedtime here is playtime on the far side of the world, where children are facing the sun.',
 know:'Earth turns once every 24 hours — that is what a day is. The spin never stops; nobody feels it because everything turns together. When your child goes to bed, families on the opposite half of the planet are sitting down to breakfast in their daytime.',
 why:'This is the set\u2019s biggest scale-up: from a garden to a spinning planet. The torch-on-a-ball model makes the invisible rotation visible and answers the night question with mechanics instead of magic — and it quietly explains time zones before bedtime.',
 dev:'Model thinking (ball as Earth, torch as sun), the swap-perspective idea (day here, morning over there), scale — from bedroom to planet — and the deep fact that a day is not the sun\u2019s doing but the ground\u2019s turning.',
 vocab:'day, night, Earth, spin, sun, torch',
 teach:'1) Show the card: what two scenes can you find — a sunny day side and a starry night side? The child is standing right between them.\n2) Ask the bedtime question: where does the sun go at night? Collect the guesses, then flip it: the sun stays put — WE turn away.\n3) Build the model: torch on, ball out. The lit half is having day, the dark half night. Now give the ball a slow turn — day sweeps across it.\n4) Spin the child: one slow full turn — that took a whole day\u2019s worth. Earth does that in exactly 24 hours, every day, without stopping.\n5) Try the other side: when it is bedtime here, children on the far side of the world are heading out to play in their morning sun. Same sun, different turn of the ball.',
 say:'"The sun does not go anywhere at night — the Earth turns! Our whole planet spins like a top, one turn every day. When our side faces the sun, it is day. As we turn away, night rolls in — and on the far side of the world, children are waking up to their morning. We are all riding the same spinning ball."',
 notice:'Ask your child: what two scenes can you find in the picture? Which side is day and which is night? Can you find the stars? What is on the sunny half of the picture?',
 ask:'"What is Earth doing right now — standing still or spinning?" "How long does one full spin take?" "Where does the sun go at night?" "When it is bedtime here, what are children on the far side doing?"',
 childSay:'"WE are the ones spinning — not the sun!" "One turn equals one whole day!" Your child may spin slowly on the spot announcing sunset and sunrise, hold a torch over a globe at bedtime, or wave goodnight to the children whose morning is starting on the far side of the world.',
 real:'The spin shows up daily: the sun\u2019s arc across the sky, shadows swinging round the garden, breakfast-afternoon-bedtime in a steady loop. When a faraway cousin sends a breakfast photo at your bedtime, your child has the answer ready: the ball keeps turning.',
 play:'Planet spin game: your child is Earth, a torch-wielding grown-up is the sun. Face the torch — "day!" — turn slowly away — "night!" — one full turn, "a whole day!" Then swap roles and let them call the light. Add a toy animal on their shoulder: does the animal see the sun all day?',
 check:'Ask: "What makes day and night? How long is one spin? Where does the sun go at night?" Earth\u2019s turning, 24 hours, nowhere — we turn away = landed.',
 tips:'Dim the room for the torch model — the lit half is much clearer. Let your child find the exact spot where their own bedroom sits on the ball and predict its light. The far-side fact lands best with a real map: pick a country, check its bedtime, wonder together.'},
'volcano':{
 title:'Volcano',one:'A mountain with a hot heart — build one in the kitchen, let a grown-up run the fizz, and compare foam to real lava.',
 seoTitle:'Volcano Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach Eruptions (Class 31)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: what a volcano is and why it erupts, what lava really is, and a grown-up-helped baking-soda eruption to run at home.',
 intro:'This lesson lets your child run an eruption — a safe one — while learning the honest version: deep underground, rock melts, pressure builds, and mountains occasionally let go. The kitchen model bubbles; the real thing creeps downhill glowing, and both are Earth showing its hot inside.',
 what:'A volcano is a mountain connected to the hot rock deep below the ground. Down there it gets so hot that rock melts; the melted rock — lava — rises, pressure builds, and sometimes the mountain erupts, pouring glowing lava down its sides until it cools hard into new rock.',
 mean:'The ground under your feet has a hot heart: the deeper you go, the hotter it gets, and volcanoes are the places where that heat sometimes reaches the top. Lava is rock so hot it flows — and when it cools, it is rock again. The kitchen model copies the flow, not the heat.',
 know:'Real lava is over a thousand degrees and creeps downhill slowly, like extremely thick honey — slow enough that people can usually walk away from it. There are about 1,500 active volcanoes on Earth, and most grumble gently, puffing steam instead of booming. Erupted lava hardens into brand-new rock.',
 why:'Volcanoes show Earth as active — the ground has an inside, and it is hot. Running the baking-soda model gives your child the predict-pour-watch thrill of experiments, and the honest comparison (foam versus lava) keeps it real without a single nightmare.',
 dev:'Cause-and-effect under pressure (build-up, eruption, flow), model-versus-reality comparison, the underground-heat idea, and experimental thrills with grown-up handles on anything hot — a first lab-safety division of labour.',
 vocab:'volcano, lava, erupt, hot, rock, pressure',
 teach:'1) Show the card: what is the mountain doing, and what color is the lava? Is the child watching from far away or close up? Name the eruption.\n2) Tell the inside story: deep underground, rock melts because of the heat; the melted rock is called lava, and it climbs and climbs until the mountain lets go.\n3) Compare flows: real lava creeps like extremely thick honey, glowing red-hot, and cools into new rock. Where do you think our foam model will flow?\n4) Run the kitchen eruption: build a dough mountain around a cup, spoon in baking soda, and let the grown-up pour the vinegar — then step back and watch it bubble over.\n5) Close the loop: our foam lava flowed downhill just like the real thing — but the real lava is rock a thousand degrees hot, which is why a grown-up handles all heat, always.',
 say:'"A volcano is a mountain with a job: when the rock deep underground melts and the pressure builds up, the mountain erupts and glowing lava pours down its sides. It creeps slowly, like the thickest honey ever — and when it cools down, it turns back into solid rock. Our kitchen volcano does the same flow with bubbles: the grown-up handles the fizz, you handle the watching."',
 notice:'Ask your child: what is the mountain doing? What color is the lava pouring down? Is the child standing close or far away? Where do you think the lava will stop?',
 ask:'"What is lava — melted what?" "Why does a volcano erupt?" "What happens to lava when it cools down?" "Who pours the vinegar in our kitchen volcano — and why?"',
 childSay:'"Lava is rock that melted!" "It cools back into rock — new rock!" Your child may rebuild the dough volcano daily, narrate eruptions with a slow honey-voice, or explain that volcanoes are "mountains letting off steam — and sometimes lava".',
 real:'Volcano evidence is in the ground everywhere: black rocky cliffs in holiday photos, hot springs and steaming vents in volcanic countries, and the island chain of Hawaii — whole islands built by cooled lava, layer on layer, still growing today.',
 play:'Dough volcano repeat lab: build the mountain, erupt, then reshape and re-run — does more baking soda make a bigger flow? Steeper mountain, faster foam? Let your child change ONE thing each round, exactly like a fair test, and crown the best eruption of the day.',
 check:'Ask: "What is lava? What happens when it cools? Who handles the hot parts of experiments?" Melted rock, it hardens into new rock, grown-ups = landed.',
 safety:'The kitchen eruption is fizz, not fire — but the honest rule stands: grown-ups handle anything hot, and real lava at over a thousand degrees is a look-at-pictures, never-go-near event. On holiday near volcanic areas, marked paths and barrier fences are there because the ground itself can be hot; feet stay on the safe side, always.',
 tips:'Film the eruption on the second run — after the surprise, your child will want to watch the foam flow frame by frame. Old dough mountains dry into permanent volcanoes for the toy shelf. Book the science-fair volcano for later; yours already works.'},
'dinosaur-fossil':{
 title:'Dinosaur Fossil',one:'Bones that turned to stone — dig chips out of a cookie and meet the buried clues that taught us dinosaurs.',
 seoTitle:'Dinosaur Fossil Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach Fossils (Class 31)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how fossils form under layers of mud, how palaeontologists dig, and a chocolate-chip cookie excavation for small hands.',
 intro:'This lesson solves a mystery your child may not have noticed yet: nobody alive has ever seen a dinosaur — so how do we know them? Buried bones, pressed to stone, dug out slowly. The cookie excavation lets your child dig like a real palaeontologist, and slow hands win.',
 what:'A fossil is the stone-hard remains of a living thing from long ago. An animal buried quickly by mud or sand skips the rotting; over millions of years, pressure turns the buried bones to rock. Dig them up, and the skeleton tells you everything.',
 mean:'Fossils are evidence, the only witnesses left: no photos, no videos, just buried clues. Scientists who read them — palaeontologists — are detectives, working out what dinosaurs ate, how big they grew and how they moved, one brush-stroke at a time.',
 know:'A fossil needs fast burial: mud or flood sand must cover the bones before they can rot, and then age plus deep pressure slowly turn them to stone. The digging is famously slow — one real bone can take weeks, brushed free grain by grain. Museums are full of the results.',
 why:'Fossils introduce deep time — millions of years — in the gentlest way, and they frame scientists as clue-readers, which is exactly the habit your child is building. The cookie dig trains the patience: real discoveries come from careful hands, not fast ones.',
 dev:'Evidence-based thinking (how we know what nobody saw), fine motor control in the excavation, patience with a slow process, and vocabulary — fossil, palaeontologist, excavation — that makes museum visits into homecomings.',
 vocab:'fossil, bones, dig, buried, stone, clues',
 teach:'1) Show the card: what is the child brushing dirt off? Can you find the dinosaur\u2019s ribs, its leg, its skull? Name the buried skeleton.\n2) Ask the mystery question: nobody alive ever saw a dinosaur — so how do we know what they looked like? The buried bones tell us.\n3) Explain the making: a dinosaur must be buried fast — under mud or flood sand — before it has time to rot; then years beyond counting press the bones slowly into stone.\n4) Set the cookie dig: treat the chocolate chips as buried fossils and the cookie as the rock around them. Dig out every chip whole with a toothpick — slow hands win, exactly like a real dig.\n5) Read the findings: lay your excavated "fossils" in a row. What kind of creature left them? That is palaeontology — reading stories told in stone.',
 say:'"Nobody has ever seen a living dinosaur — but we know them anyway, because of fossils. When a dinosaur died and got buried quickly by mud, its bones stayed put for millions of years while the pressing turned them to stone. Scientists dig them out with tiny brushes, so slowly that one bone can take weeks. The bones are the story."',
 notice:'Ask your child: what is the child brushing? What is hiding under the dirt? Can you find the bones — where is the skull, where is a leg? Is the child brushing fast or slowly?',
 ask:'"What is a fossil?" "How do we know about dinosaurs if nobody saw them?" "Why must bones be buried quickly?" "What does a palaeontologist do all day?"',
 childSay:'"Fossils are bones turned to STONE!" "Slow hands win — like a real palaeontologist!" Your child may brush every flowerbed carefully on the lookout for buried clues, lay toys in mud "for the future to find", or explain that museums are full of "stone bones".',
 real:'Fossils sit in plain reach: ammonite shapes in beach stones, fossil-hunting beaches with chalk cliffs, museum halls where a real T. rex skull leans over the rail. Even a garden pebble with a shell print is a fossil clue — the past, pressing into the present.',
 play:'Cookie excavation dig site: one chocolate-chip cookie, one toothpick, one small brush per palaeontologist. Rule of the site: every chip out whole, no crumbling the "rock". Then record the find — draw the chips in a grid, like a real dig map. Repeat until the site is exhausted and the palaeontologist is snack-full.',
 check:'Ask: "What is a fossil? How did the bones turn to stone? Who digs them up slowly?" Stone-hard old bones, pressing for ages, palaeontologists = landed.',
 tips:'The toothpick-and-brush combo matters — the brush does the final cleaning, just like real digs, and the two tools slow the digging to science speed. If a natural history museum is within reach, go within a fortnight: your child will walk in naming the palaeontologist\u2019s job out loud.'},
'magnet-attracts-metal':{
 title:'What Magnets Attract',one:'Stick or no stick? Guess first, then test everything in the house and sort the winners from the duds.',
 seoTitle:'What Magnets Attract Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach Stick Tests (Class 31)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: which things a magnet sticks to and why, with a predict-then-test ten-object house hunt sorted into STICKS and NO piles.',
 intro:'This lesson is the perfect first experiment: a magnet, ten household objects, and one question — will it stick? Your child predicts, tests and sorts, and discovers that "metal" is not the answer: only iron and steel stick, and the surprises are the lesson.',
 what:'Magnets pull — but only on certain things. Iron and steel hold fast; wood, plastic, glass, paper and fabric stay stubborn. Many everyday "metal" objects — most coins, foil and drink cans — are metals a magnet ignores. The magnet is the only honest judge.',
 mean:'Not all metal is magnetic: the pull works on iron and steel, which is why the fridge door holds a drawing and the aluminium can does not. Sorting stick from no-stick is classification, and the magnet does the sorting instantly — no opinions needed.',
 know:'A magnet always sticks to iron and steel — spoons, keys, radiators, the fridge door. It never sticks to wood, plastic, glass, paper or cloth, no matter how hard you wish. Foil and most coins fail the test: they are metals, just not the magnetic kind.',
 why:'This is the full predict-test-conclude cycle in one safe game: your child guesses before touching, tests with the magnet, and sorts results into piles. Being wrong out loud — the spoon stuck, the coin did not — is exactly how experiments teach.',
 dev:'Prediction before testing (the scientist\u2019s core habit), sorting into two clean categories, vocabulary for materials (iron, steel, plastic, wood, metal), and the surprise-mindset: wrong guesses are findings, not failures.',
 vocab:'magnet, stick, metal, steel, test, sort',
 teach:'1) Show the card: what is in the child\u2019s hand, and what shape does it have? Can you count the paperclips waiting nearby? Name the tool — a horseshoe magnet.\n2) Set the game: guess FIRST, test SECOND. Will the spoon stick? The coin? The toy car? Call every guess out loud before the magnet touches anything.\n3) Run the house hunt: ten objects, one magnet — spoons, coins, keys, toys, zips — even the door handle. Stick or no?\n4) Sort the verdicts: two piles on the table — STICKS on the left, NO on the right. What did the stickers share? Iron and steel, mostly hiding inside things.\n5) Land the surprise: coins and foil are metal but do NOT stick — not all metal is magnetic. The magnet is the only judge that never gets it wrong.',
 say:'"A magnet is a picky puller — it only sticks to iron and steel. Wooden spoons? No. Plastic bricks? No. The metal zip? Sometimes yes! Before we test, we guess — that is what scientists do. Then the magnet tells the truth, and into the piles it goes: STICKS, and no."',
 notice:'Ask your child: what is the child holding? What shape is the magnet? Can you find all the paperclips — how many are there? Do you think they will all stick?',
 ask:'"Does a magnet stick to wood?" "What kinds of metal do magnets pull?" "What do we do first — guess or test?" "What did the coin do when we tested it?"',
 childSay:'"The coin did NOT stick — but the spoon did!" "Guess first, then the magnet tells the truth!" Your child may test every fridge door in the house, walk the kitchen with a magnet like a metal detector, or announce "not all metal sticks!" at unrelated moments for weeks.',
 real:'Stick tests are everywhere once the magnet is out: the radiator (sticks), the mirror frame (often not), zip pulls, bath toys, the cutlery drawer. The fridge door becomes a daily test station — and gift shops sell magnet sets that keep the hunt going for years.',
 play:'Mystery-bag magnetism: fill a cloth bag with safe household objects — some steel, some not. Your child predicts what is inside, then fishes with a magnet through the fabric: sticks means steel, no means mystery unsolved — pull it out and check. Sorting, predicting, surprise: the full loop.',
 check:'Ask: "What do magnets stick to? Do all metals stick? What comes first — the guess or the test?" Iron and steel, no, the guess = landed.',
 safety:'Small magnets and small parts are a baby-sibling hazard: swallowed magnets can pinch inside the body and cause real harm, so magnet play happens up high, out of baby territory, and every magnet gets counted back at the end. Big snap-together magnets also pinch fingers hard — little hands use big magnets, gently.',
 tips:'Let your child keep the two sorted piles for a day and re-test anything that objects to its verdict — re-testing is real science. Fridge-magnet letters make a bonus round: they stick because their backs hide soft iron.'},
'magnet':{
 title:'Magnet Force',one:'Pull, push and through-things power — feel two magnets argue, then watch a paperclip lift through solid paper.',
 seoTitle:'Magnet Force Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach Pull and Push (Class 31)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: magnetic pull and push with no touching, north and south poles, and a feel-the-force two-magnet game with paper barriers.',
 intro:'This lesson goes one step past what sticks: force with no contact at all. Your child feels the invisible pull when two magnets meet, the invisible cushion when they argue, and the strangest trick of all — a paperclip lifting while paper stands in between.',
 what:'A magnet can move things without touching them: it pulls steel from across a gap, and it grips hardest at its two ends — the poles. Turn two magnets the wrong way round and they push apart, no hands needed. The force even works through paper, water and a table top.',
 mean:'Force at a distance is the wild part: nothing is touching, yet something moves. Every magnet has a north and a south pole, and the rule is absolute — opposite poles pull together, matching poles push apart. The invisible cushion your child feels in their palm is a push they can trust.',
 know:'Both ends of a magnet are poles — north and south. Opposite poles grab each other; matching poles shove apart. No exceptions, ever. Magnetic force reaches through paper, wood, water and skin, but it fades fast: twice as far away means much less pull.',
 why:'Feeling a push with no visible cause stretches the invisible-things-are-real idea from wind to a new level — force without contact. It also hands your child a rule with no exceptions (opposites pull, matches push) and the joy of a power they can hold in one hand.',
 dev:'Feeling invisible forces directly (pull, push, through-things), rule-testing (flip the magnet, check the rule), vocabulary — poles, attract, repel — and early physics wonder: this same invisible-force idea grows up into gravity and electricity.',
 vocab:'pull, push, poles, force, attract, distance',
 teach:'1) Show the card: what is hanging under the magnet, and what is the child lifting with no hands? Name the trick — force with no fingers.\n2) Feel the pull: hold two magnets loosely, ends toward each other — let them snap together and feel the grab. That force crossed the gap with no touching.\n3) Feel the push: flip one magnet and try again — the same ends shove apart like an invisible cushion. Opposite poles pull, matching poles push. Always.\n4) Break the barrier: slip a sheet of paper between the two — the clip still lifts! The force goes through things. Try a table top, a book, a cup of water.\n5) Map the poles: name the two ends north and south, test all four combinations, and let your child announce the rule like a law of nature — because it is one.',
 say:'"A magnet moves things with no touching at all — that is its superpower. It grips hardest at its two ends, the poles. Turn two magnets so opposite poles meet and they grab each other. Flip one around and they push apart — you can feel the invisible cushion! And the force works through paper and even water. Try it: nothing touches, and things still move."',
 notice:'Ask your child: what is hanging from the magnet? Is the magnet holding the paperclip from above? What colors can you find on the magnet? What is the child lifting without fingers?',
 ask:'"What are the two ends of a magnet called?" "What do opposite poles do — pull or push?" "Can magnet force work through paper?" "What did you feel when two matching poles met?"',
 childSay:'"Opposites attract — matching poles push apart!" "I felt the invisible cushion — it pushed my hand away!" Your child may float magnets across the table like a duel, test whether the force works through their sleeve, or explain that poles "are the strongest bits of the magnet".',
 real:'Magnetic force is at work all over the house: the fridge seal that clicks shut, cabinet latches, the magnetic clasp on a bag, phone cases that snap on. Train sets and magnetic tiles run on the same poles rule your child just felt in the palm of a hand.',
 play:'Magnet maze: draw a maze on card, lay a paperclip "racer" on top, and steer it through from below with a bar magnet — no touching the racer, force through the card only. Then race: two racers, two magnets, first to the finish without lifting the magnet. Steady poles win.',
 check:'Ask: "What do opposite poles do? What do matching poles do? Can the force work through paper?" Pull together, push apart, yes — through things = landed.',
 safety:'Strong magnets are a small-parts danger for baby siblings: if two are swallowed they can pinch tissue inside the body and cause serious harm — magnet play stays up high, and every piece is counted back in. Big snap-together magnets also pinch fingers viciously; little hands use big magnets, slowly.',
 tips:'The maze needs a roomy paperclip and slow hands — steer from below, wrists low. For the water trick, drop a steel spoon into a cup and run the magnet up the outside: the spoon follows through the wall. Matching-pole pushes deserve a rerun whenever the mood strikes; the invisible cushion never gets old.'},
'sound':{
 title:'Sound',one:'Every sound is a shaking — make rice dance on a drum, hum in your throat and feel the wobble that hears.',
 seoTitle:'Sound Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach Vibrations (Class 31)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how shaking things make sound, why big drums boom, and a rice-on-a-drum experiment that lets your child see sound jump.',
 intro:'This lesson makes sound touchable from both ends: the shaking that makes it — visible as rice dancing on a drum skin — and the shaking that hears it, felt as a buzz in your own throat. Quiet and loud, deep and squeaky, all come down to one wobbling idea.',
 what:'Every sound starts with a shake: a drum skin wobbles, the shake rides waves through the air to your ear, and the last wobble lands on your eardrum — which wobbles too. That final wobble is hearing. No shaking, no sound.',
 mean:'Sound is movement you can see and feel, not just hear: rice jumps on a drum, your throat buzzes when you hum, a big slow wobble sounds deep and a fast one sounds squeaky. Quiet means small shakes; loud means big ones — volume is shaking size.',
 know:'Bigger things wobble slower, and slow wobbles sound deep — that is why a big drum booms while a small one taps. Sound can travel through your bones too: tuck your fingertips lightly over your ears and say something, and your own voice arrives loud and clear from the inside.',
 why:'Sound turns an invisible everyday thing into a testable one: your child can see the rice jump, feel the throat buzz, and hear the difference a size makes. It also explains the volume knob, the quiet game, and why libraries whisper — all one wobbling idea.',
 dev:'Multisensory evidence (seeing, feeling, hearing the same shake), cause-and-effect with size and pitch, impulse control through quiet-loud games, and the body knowledge that your own throat and ears are vibration equipment.',
 vocab:'sound, vibration, drum, loud, quiet, wobble',
 teach:'1) Show the card: what is the child playing, and what is rippling out from the drum? Name it — sound waves, made by shaking.\n2) Feel it first: fingers on the throat, hum like a bee. That buzz is the vibration — you ARE the sound machine.\n3) Make it visible: stretch a balloon skin tight over a bowl, dust it with dry rice, and tap a metal pot loudly next to it. Every boom, the rice jumps — the shake, made seeable.\n4) Sort the sizes: big drum, slow deep wobble — boom. Small drum, fast tiny wobble — tap. Loud is a big shake, quiet is a small one.\n5) Travel the sound: tuck fingertips over your ears and talk — the sound sneaks through your own bones. Then whisper: how small can a wobble get?',
 say:'"Every sound starts as a wobble — a shake! The drum skin shakes, the shake rides the air all the way to your ear, and your eardrum shakes back: that is hearing. Put your fingers on your throat and hum — feel it? You are making a wobble right now. Big slow wobbles boom, tiny fast ones squeak."',
 notice:'Ask your child: what is the child playing? What is rippling out from the drum? Can you pretend to tap a drum? Does the sound look like it is going far away or staying close?',
 ask:'"What is every sound made of?" "What did the rice do when the drum boomed?" "Why does a big drum sound deep?" "How can you hear a sound with your fingers over your ears?"',
 childSay:'"Sound is a wobble that your ear catches!" "The rice is jumping — I can SEE the sound!" Your child may hum with fingers on throat to prove the buzz, tap pots gently near the rice drum to make it dance, or whisper "small wobble" instead of quiet.',
 real:'Sound shakes are everywhere: a speaker\u2019s cone you can feel humming, a window buzzing when a lorry passes, the purr of a cat against your hand. The volume game works in every room — big shakes for the playground, small shakes for the library, and now your child knows why.',
 play:'Rice-drum rock band: one bowl with a balloon-skin or cling-film top, rice sprinkled, and a pot-and-spoon kit for drums. Play loud beside it and watch the rice mosh, then tiptoe the taps — barely a grain moves. Chart it: boom, jump; whisper, still. Sound, sized.',
 check:'Ask: "What makes every sound? What does loud mean for the shaking? What did the rice show us?" Wobbling, big shakes, the vibration = landed.',
 tips:'Balloon skins beat cling film for bounce — stretch tight and tape well. Keep the rice on the drum, not the floor, with a tray underneath. The ears-and-speak trick is a reliable wow: your child\u2019s own voice, arriving through bone. Follow with a quiet walk and a hunt for the smallest wobbles they can hear.'},
'shadow':{
 title:'Shadow',one:'The dark shape where you stop the light — chase your shadow at noon, at dusk and behind the torch beam.',
 seoTitle:'Shadow Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach Shadow Play (Class 31)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how shadows form when something blocks the light, why they stretch and shrink, and hand-puppet shadow play for evenings.',
 intro:'This lesson turns a puddle of dark on the pavement into light physics: your child\u2019s body stops the sunshine, and the shape of the stopping follows them all day — long at breakfast, short at noon, gone after dark, and reborn behind a torch for puppet shows.',
 what:'A shadow is the dark shape made where something blocks light: your body stops the sunshine on its way to the ground, and the dark patch that results is shaped exactly like the stopping — you. Move, and the shadow moves; more light or less light, and it stretches or shrinks.',
 mean:'Light travels in straight lines, and it cannot go through you — so behind you there is a light-shaped hole in the daylight. Shadows are always on the side away from the light: sun in front, shadow behind. No light, no shadow — a cloudy night makes none at all.',
 know:'Your shadow runs longest when the sun hangs low in the sky — breakfast time, supper time — and shrinks to almost nothing at midday, when it crouches right under your feet. Shadow puppets work because the torch beam is a straight line: block it with a hand, and the wall shows the hand\u2019s shape, huge.',
 why:'Shadows are physics your child can make with their own body, chase on any sunny day, and command behind a torch. The changing length teaches that the sun moves across the sky — or really, that we turn — and shadow play turns blocking light into theatre.',
 dev:'Observation over a whole day (long, short, gone), positional reasoning (shadow always points away from the light), creative hand-eye play in puppet form, and the seed of the day-and-night lesson: light, blocked and moving.',
 vocab:'shadow, light, sun, torch, block, shape',
 teach:'1) Show the card: where is the child standing, and can you find the shadow? Is it long or short? Name it — the shape of the light being stopped.\n2) Explain the making: light travels in straight lines and cannot pass through your body, so behind you lands a dark patch shaped exactly like the blocker — you.\n3) Chase it outside: morning shadow stretches long, noon shadow tucks short underfoot. Draw a chalk line and measure your shadow at both ends of the day.\n4) Ask the direction rule: where does the sun stand? Your shadow points the other way — always away from the light.\n5) Stage the puppet show: darkish room, torch on the wall, hands making birds and dogs. Block the beam, and the wall plays back your shape — bigger than you are.',
 say:'"Your shadow is the dark shape where your body stops the light. The sunshine cannot go through you, so it lands behind you as a you-shaped patch. In the morning it stretches long, at noon it hides short under your feet, and at night — no sun, no shadow. But a torch will build you a new one any time you want."',
 notice:'Ask your child: where is the child standing? Can you find the child\u2019s shadow? Is the shadow long or short? Which way is the shadow pointing — toward the sun, or away from it?',
 ask:'"What makes a shadow?" "When is your shadow longest — morning or noon?" "Which way does a shadow point?" "What do you need for a shadow puppet show after dark?"',
 childSay:'"My shadow is me, blocking the light!" "At noon it hides under my feet!" Your child may jump to see if the shadow jumps (it does), measure shadows in chalk all summer, or put on a nightly hand-puppet show that begins the moment the torch comes out.',
 real:'Shadows mark the day without a clock: the long breakfast shadow, the vanishing noon one, the stretched evening giant. Watch out for the reversible surprise — a cloudy day has no shadow, but any lamp, phone torch or streetlight will build one on demand, indoors or out.',
 play:'Shadow theatre: a bedsheet screen, a bright torch behind it, and hand puppets — bird (thumbs hooked, fingers flapping), dog (thumb ears), rabbit (two-finger ears). Let your child direct the show, then swap: their hands become the actors. Move the torch closer — the animals grow. Why?',
 check:'Ask: "What makes a shadow? Which way does it point? When is it longest?" Blocked light, away from the light, low-sun morning and evening = landed.',
 tips:'The chalk-line shadow clock is the two-day project that keeps giving: trace the same spot\u2019s shadow at morning, noon and evening, then repeat next season. Torch puppets need one dim room and one bright beam — and a patient audience of toys. Shadows point away from the light: test it with two lamps and a toy.'},
'feather':{
 title:'Feather',one:'A bird\u2019s all-in-one gear — drop a feather against a stone, see the air catch it, and find the tiny hooks inside.',
 seoTitle:'Feather Lesson for Ages 4–5 — How to Teach Feathers (Class 31)',
 metaDescription:'A Learning Path lesson for ages 4–5: how feathers fly birds and keep them warm, with a feather-versus-stone drop race and a real-feather close look to try.',
 intro:'This lesson answers two bird questions with one object: how do birds fly, and how do they stay warm? Your child races a feather against a stone, watches the air catch every strand, and looks close enough to find the tiny hooks that zip a bird\u2019s coat shut.',
 what:'Feathers are a bird\u2019s built-in gear: long flight feathers catch the air for flying, and soft fluffy down feathers trap warm air close to the body like a duvet. A bird can have thousands of them, zipped neat with its beak.',
 mean:'The feather wins the drop race because the air catches it: light, wide and spread into strands, it falls slow — while the stone drops straight through. Down works the same trick for warmth: feathers trap air, and trapped air is the best blanket there is.',
 know:'Birds preen — zipping their feathers tidy with their beaks, like combing a coat. Flight feathers are built from a hollow stem with strands branching off, and those strands hold tiny hooks that clip together. Your winter jacket and sleeping bag copy the fluffiest feathers, strand for strand.',
 why:'The feather-versus-stone race is a fair test your child can run off any park bench — and the answer (air catches it) links feathers to flying. Finding hooks on a real feather shows that gear can be tiny, and the jacket connection brings bird science to the coat rack.',
 dev:'Fair testing (same height, drop together), cause-and-effect with air resistance at playground scale, close observation with a magnifier, and the design idea: birds and coat makers solved the same problem the same way.',
 vocab:'feather, bird, fly, warm, down, air',
 teach:'1) Show the card: what is the child holding — soft or scratchy? What color is the feather? Name its two jobs: flying and warm-keeping.\n2) Run the drop race: feather against a small stone, released together from the same height. Which floats, which thuds? The air grabs every feather strand.\n3) Explain the flying: wide flight feathers push against the air like oars — a bird flaps, catches air, and stays up. Stone could never.\n4) Tell the warm secret: under the flight feathers sits fluffy down that traps warm air — your winter jacket is stuffed with the same idea.\n5) Look close on a real feather: smooth vane on one side, fluffy on the other, and along the strands — tiny hooks that zip the whole thing shut.',
 say:'"A feather does two jobs for a bird: the long ones catch the air for flying, and the fluffy ones hold warm air like a duvet. Watch — drop a feather and the air catches it, strand by strand, so it floats down slow. The stone has no such trick; it just drops. And your winter coat? It copies the fluffy kind."',
 notice:'Ask your child: what is the child holding? Is the feather soft or hard? What color is it? Who do you think the feather belonged to?',
 ask:'"What are a bird\u2019s two kinds of feathers for?" "Why does a feather fall slower than a stone?" "What do birds do with their beaks to keep feathers neat?" "What does your winter coat copy from birds?"',
 childSay:'"The air catches the feather — that is why it floats!" "My coat has down — bird stuff, copied!" Your child may drop leaves, scarves and paper alongside stones all week, preen their own hair in front of the mirror like a bird, or inspect every found feather for hooks.',
 real:'Feather signs are everywhere: the pigeon feather by the bus stop, the duvet on your child\u2019s bed, the duck shaking water off its back — water rolls off because preened feathers are zipped tight. The park is a feather showroom; the coat rack is the copycat aisle.',
 play:'Feather-versus-anything Olympics: race a feather against a coin, a leaf, a scarf square and a crumpled paper ball — same height, drop on the count of three. Rank the floaters, then untie the mystery: wide and light floats, small and heavy drops. The air is the judge.',
 check:'Ask: "What do feathers do for birds? Why does a feather float down slowly? What is your winter coat stuffed with?" Fly and warm, the air catches it, down = landed.',
 tips:'Found feathers get a gentle wash-and-dry before close looking — and a magnifier turns the hooks from a claim into a sighting. Shop feathers work for the race too, but a real one shows the zip. If a bird lets you watch it preen, you have seen the whole lesson in ten seconds.'}
};
